DIAGRAM('craft-save-systems', { kind:'flow', title:'A save that survives a crash', steps:[
  { id:'s', t:'State to bytes', d:'with a version number' }, { id:'t', t:'Write a temp file' }, { id:'f', t:'Flush to disk' }, { id:'r', t:'Rename over the old', d:'atomic' }],
  edges:[['s','t'],['t','f'],['f','r']] });

T('craft-measuring-ai-uplift',{ d:'craft', t:'Measuring whether AI helps your team', tag:'The studies disagree, and people misjudge their own speed. Measure your own team on your own code, with review time and stability counted.',
  what:`A method for finding out whether an AI coding tool makes your team faster or better on your codebase, instead of trusting a study, a vendor or a feeling. The published evidence points in different directions, and each study is narrow. In METR’s randomised trial (July 2025) 16 experienced open-source developers worked 246 tasks in repositories they had maintained for about five years. Tasks were randomly allowed or not allowed to use AI (mostly Cursor with Claude 3.5 and 3.7). With AI, tasks took 19% longer (interval +2% to +39%). Before starting, developers forecast a 24% speedup; afterwards they still believed AI had made them 20% faster. In three field experiments at Microsoft, Accenture and a Fortune 100 firm (4,867 developers, Cui et al., 2025), Copilot access raised completed tasks by 26.08% (standard error 10.3%), and less experienced developers gained more. In an earlier lab task, writing an HTTP server in JavaScript, the Copilot group finished 55.8% faster (Peng et al., 2023). These are not contradictions to be settled by picking a side. They measure different people, tasks and tool generations: a bounded new task, autocomplete-era tools and a large mature repository are not the same problem. METR’s own February 2026 update is weaker evidence and points the other way: in a redesigned study of 57 developers and 800+ tasks, returning developers were estimated 18% faster (interval -38% to +9% in time) and new developers 4% faster (-15% to +9%). METR calls this only very weak evidence, because developers who did not want to work without AI opted out, 30 to 50% of developers said they held back some tasks they did not want to do without it, and agents running in parallel broke the time tracking. The honest reading is: the speed effect on experienced people in mature code is unsettled and tool-dependent, so you measure locally. Two more results say what to measure. In a study of 806 GitHub repositories that adopted Cursor against 1,380 matched controls (He et al., 2025), lines added rose 281% in the first month and 48% in the second, then the effect faded, while static-analysis warnings rose about 30% and code complexity about 42% and stayed up. And the DORA reports, which are correlational surveys, found in 2024 that a 25% rise in AI adoption went with 1.5% lower delivery throughput and 7.2% lower stability, and in 2025 that throughput was positively related to AI use while stability was still negatively related; DORA’s summary is that AI amplifies what a team already does. Output counts (lines, pull requests) rise easily and prove little.`,
  why:[`Nobody, including you, can tell from the inside. METR’s developers believed they were 20% faster when they were measured 19% slower, a 39-point gap, and a feeling of speed is not a measurement.`,`Studies disagree because their settings differ, so no study can answer "will this help my team on this codebase". Only your own comparison can.`,`Counting the wrong thing gives a false yes. Lines added jumped 281% and the gain was gone two months later, with more warnings and complexity left behind.`,`Money, licences and team habits follow the answer. A wrong yes buys tools that add review load; a wrong no throws away real gains on the kind of work where they exist.`],
  think:{ q:[`What decision will this measurement change? If none, do not run it.`,`Which tasks are in scope: greenfield, small fixes, or changes deep in code only your team knows? Each may give a different answer.`,`Who is deciding whether a task is done: the author, or a reviewer who does not know which arm it was in?`,`Do the metrics include review time, rework, escaped defects and build stability, or only time to open a pull request?`,`How many tasks can you actually run, and could that number show an effect of the size you care about?`,`What would the result have to be for you to change your mind?`],
    trade:[`Random assignment of tasks (AI allowed or not) gives cleaner evidence and annoys people who want to use the tool everywhere. Before-and-after comparisons are easier and blame the tool for everything else that changed.`,`Time tracking is only trustworthy if people are not running several agents at once; METR’s update lost accuracy exactly there.`,`A longer trial covers learning effects and novelty wearing off and costs more delivery time.`,`Outcome metrics (change failure, escaped bugs) are the ones that matter and the slowest to accumulate; leading metrics (review time) come sooner and are only a proxy.`],
    traps:[`Asking people how much faster they feel. The 2025 trial shows the answer can be wrong by a wide margin, and in the wrong direction.`,`Using lines, commits or pull-request counts as productivity. Cui et al. counted completed tasks and commits, which say nothing about quality, and He et al. show a volume gain fading within two months.`,`Letting developers pick which tasks get AI. They will pick the ones that suit it, and the comparison becomes a comparison of task choice.`,`Reading a small trial as a verdict. With 246 tasks, METR’s interval still ran from +2% to +39%; a 20-task trial can only detect a very large effect.`,`Measuring once. Tools, models and habits change every few months; a result has a shelf life.`,`Treating the DORA numbers as causes. They are associations from surveys.`],
    good:[`A written plan with the question, the tasks, the arms and the metrics agreed before the first task starts.`,`Review time, rework and post-merge defects are counted next to cycle time.`,`The result states its sample size and interval and is repeated after a tool or model change.`],
    bad:[`A slide that says "40% faster" from a survey question.`,`A tool rolled out to everyone with no baseline to compare against.`] },
  how:[`Write the question and the decision it feeds: "Should the gameplay team keep paying for agent tools, and for which task types?" Pick the outcomes before you see any data.`,`Sort your backlog into task types (bug fix in familiar code, new feature in an old system, tool script, new prototype). Analyse each type separately; a blended number hides where the tool helps.`,`Assign at random by task, not by person: for each eligible task, a coin flip or a script decides "AI allowed" or "no AI". Record the assignment before work begins. If a strict ban is not acceptable, use a pair of similar tasks and randomise which gets the tool.`,`Record, per task: start and finish, time waiting for review, review comments, rework commits after review, and whether the change was reverted or caused a bug within a fixed window (for example 30 days). Also record the estimate made beforehand, so you can see forecast against result.`,`Add stability signals at team level: failed builds, reverted merges, hot fixes, the static-analysis warning count and a complexity measure on the touched code. Compare before and after, since these move slowly.`,`Ask people afterwards how fast they felt, and put that next to the measured numbers. The gap is worth reporting to the team.`,`Report the sample size, a range and what the sample cannot say (one team, one codebase, one tool version). If the range includes zero, say "not shown", not "no effect".`,`Set a review date. Re-run a short version when the tool, the model or the main task type changes.`],
  ai:{ yes:[`Draft the task classification and the data-collection sheet.`,`Compute summary statistics and intervals from the numbers you give it, then have a person re-check the calculation.`,`Summarise review comments by type so rework can be compared across arms.`],
       no:[`Rate its own contribution to a task. It has no reliable view of time saved.`,`Decide which tasks are in the AI arm. Randomisation must not depend on anyone’s judgement of suitability.`] },
  prompts:[{l:'Trial plan',p:`We want to know whether [TOOL] helps our [TEAM] on [CODEBASE]. Draft a paired-task trial plan: task types, how tasks are randomly assigned, what to record per task (time, review wait, rework, defects within 30 days), the team-level stability metrics, and the smallest effect our sample of [N] tasks could detect. List the ways the trial could mislead us.`},
    {l:'Metric audit',p:`Here are the metrics we plan to report: [LIST]. For each, say whether it can rise while quality or delivery gets worse, and what to pair it with so a rise is not read as a win.`}],
  verify:[`Was the assignment to AI or no-AI random and recorded before the task started?`,`Do the metrics include review time and post-merge defects, not only time to first pull request?`,`Does the report give the sample size and a range, and name what the sample cannot show?`,`Is there a date to repeat it?`],
  test:[`Run the trial analysis on shuffled arm labels. If it still "finds" a clear effect, the method or the arithmetic is broken.`,`Take last quarter’s data and count what a lines-added metric and a stability metric each say. If they disagree, you have seen why one number is not enough.`],
  facts:[{claim:`METR randomised trial (Becker, Rush, Barnes, Rein; July 2025): 16 experienced open-source developers, 246 tasks in mature repositories; with AI allowed, tasks took 19% longer (interval +2% to +39% in the February 2026 revision). Developers forecast 24% faster and afterwards believed 20% faster. The authors say experimental artifacts cannot be entirely ruled out.`,asOf:'2026-09-30',src:'https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.09089'},
    {claim:`METR, "Uplift update" (24 February 2026): a redesigned study of 57 developers, 143 repositories and 800+ tasks estimated returning developers 18% faster (interval -38% to +9% in time) and new developers 4% faster (-15% to +9%). METR calls it only very weak evidence because of selection (developers unwilling to work without AI opted out; 30 to 50% of developers said they held back some tasks they did not want to do without AI) and broken time tracking with concurrent agents.`,asOf:'2026-09-30',src:'https://metr.org/blog/2026-02-24-uplift-update/'},
    {claim:`Cui et al., three field experiments at Microsoft, Accenture and a Fortune 100 firm (4,867 developers, Copilot, 2 to 8 months): +26.08% completed tasks (SE 10.3%; the paper counts these as weekly pull requests), +13.55% commits (SE 10.0%, not clearly different from zero), +38.38% compiles (SE 12.55%); less experienced developers adopted more and gained more. Quality and agentic tools were not measured.`,asOf:'2026-09-30',src:'https://economics.mit.edu/sites/default/files/inline-files/draft_copilot_experiments.pdf'},
    {claim:`Peng et al. (February 2023): in a controlled experiment on one task, writing an HTTP server in JavaScript, the Copilot group finished 55.8% faster.`,asOf:'2026-09-30',src:'https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.06590'},
    {claim:`He et al., "Speed at the Cost of Quality" (November 2025, MSR 2026): 806 GitHub repositories adopting Cursor against 1,380 matched controls, January 2024 to August 2025. Lines added +281.3% in month one and +48.4% in month two, then faded; static-analysis warnings rose about 30% and code complexity about 42%, both persisting. Observational, open source only, adoption inferred from a committed Cursor config file.`,asOf:'2026-09-30',src:'https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.04427'},
    {claim:`DORA 2024 (survey, association not cause): a 25% rise in AI adoption went with -1.5% delivery throughput and -7.2% delivery stability. DORA 2025 (about 5,000 respondents plus interviews): throughput now positively related to AI use, stability still negatively related; AI described as an amplifier of existing team capabilities.`,asOf:'2026-09-30',src:'https://cloud.google.com/blog/products/ai-machine-learning/announcing-the-2025-dora-report'},
    {claim:`Stack Overflow Developer Survey 2025 (33,662 people answered the AI-use question; the survey had 49,000+ responses in all): 84% use or plan to use AI tools; 66% say AI answers are "almost right, but not quite"; 45.2% say debugging AI-generated code takes longer. Self-reported, self-selected respondents.`,asOf:'2026-09-30',src:'https://survey.stackoverflow.co/2025/ai'}],
  rel:[['craft-engineer-in-the-ai-era','That topic states the skill; this one gives the method for checking the claim about your own team.'],['craft-verification-as-the-job','Stability and defect counts are verification results; the trial needs them.'],['craft-what-matters-now','Measure the things that matter, not the ones that rise easily.'],['models-reasoning-and-scaling','Benchmarks describe models; a local trial describes your work.'],['lead-code-review','Review time is part of the cost the trial must count.']] });

INTERVIEW('craft-measuring-ai-uplift',{
  junior:[
    { q:`Developers in a 2025 trial believed AI made them 20% faster, and it had made them 19% slower. What does that tell you about asking your team if a tool helps?`,
      a:`That how fast something feels is not a measurement. People remember the easy typing and forget the time spent checking and fixing. Ask, but also record real times and outcomes.`,
      follow:`Does that study prove AI slows developers down?`,
      red:`Says the study proves AI slows everyone, or that self-reports are fine.` }],
  mid:[
    { q:`A manager says pull requests per week doubled after the team got an agent, so it works. What do you say?`,
      a:`Counts of pull requests go up easily and say little about value. I would ask what happened to review time, reverts, bugs after merge and build failures. A study of Cursor-adopting repositories found lines added jumped and then faded within two months while warnings and complexity stayed higher.`,
      follow:`What would you measure instead?`,
      red:`Accepts volume as productivity.` },
    { q:`Two studies say +26% and -19%. How do you decide what to do?`,
      a:`They measured different things: mostly autocomplete-era tools and many developers on tasks in general, against experienced developers in large mature repositories with a different tool generation. A later METR redesign pointed the other way and was called weak evidence by its authors. So I would run a small randomised comparison on our own tasks, split by task type, and repeat it when tools change.`,
      follow:`How many tasks would you need?`,
      red:`Picks the study that fits their preference.` },
    { q:`What is the difference between a study of a lab task and a trial in your own codebase?`,
      a:`A lab task such as writing a small web server is bounded and new. Your codebase has history, conventions and hidden rules that the tool does not know. The first can show a large speedup, as one 2023 experiment did (55.8%), and the second can show none. So neither transfers automatically.`,
      follow:`What would make you trust your own trial?`,
      red:`Treats any published percentage as applying to their team.` },
    { q:`Your trial shows a 10% speedup with an interval from -30% to +15%. What do you report?`,
      a:`That the result is not shown: the range includes no effect and a slowdown. I report the task count, the range and the limits, and I do not say AI helps or does not. Then I decide whether more tasks are worth running or whether the tool is worth keeping on other grounds, and set a date to repeat it.`,
      follow:`How would you get a narrower range?`,
      red:`Reports 10% faster and drops the range.` }],
  senior:[
    { q:`Design a six-week trial for a team of eight deciding on an agent tool.`,
      a:`State the decision first. Classify tasks by type. Randomise each eligible task to AI or no AI and record the assignment before work starts. Record time, review wait, rework, defects within a fixed window, plus team-level build stability and warning counts. Ask for estimates beforehand and feelings afterwards. Report the number of tasks, a range and the limits, and say plainly if the range includes zero. Agree in advance what result changes the decision.`,
      follow:`People object to being banned from the tool on half the tasks. What do you do?`,
      red:`Compares before and after with no control, or lets people choose which tasks get the tool.` }
  ] });

T('craft-security-review-of-generated-code',{ d:'craft', t:'Security review of generated code', tag:'Model output is untrusted input that looks confident. Check trust boundaries first: what the client says, what a file contains, what the network sends.',
  what:`How to review code an AI wrote for security holes, with a review order aimed at games. The direction of the evidence is consistent, although most of it is old or comes from vendors, so it is a reason to look, not a measured rate for today’s models. Pearce et al. (2021) generated 1,689 programs from 89 scenarios based on the MITRE top-25 weaknesses and found about 40% contained vulnerabilities. Perry et al. (Stanford, 2022, revised 2023) ran a user study with 47 participants (33 with an assistant, 14 without; two-thirds undergraduates) on five security tasks. The assistant group wrote less secure code on most tasks (for example only 3% secure against 21% for message signing, and 12% against 29% for file access) and were more likely to rate their insecure answer as secure. Those who distrusted the assistant and reworded their prompts did better. Veracode’s 2025 report, a vendor’s own test of more than 100 models on Java, JavaScript, Python and C# against OWASP-style tasks, says 45% of samples failed its security tests, with cross-site scripting defended in only 14% of relevant cases, and that larger, newer models were not more secure. Treat that as a vendor claim: the pages read for this topic did not state the task count or how prompts were chosen. What this supports: generated code needs the same security review as an unknown contractor’s code, the author’s confidence carries no information, and prompting for security and reviewing the result work better than assuming either. What it does not show: how often current models fail on your kind of code, or whether professionals with review catch what students missed. The rest of this topic is the site’s approach and rule of thumb, not tested practice. In games, the main surfaces are: trust in the client (anything the player’s machine says can be edited); data read from disk or the network, including saves, mods, replays and level files; purchases and store receipts; secrets and keys shipped inside the build; dependencies an assistant suggested; and anti-cheat that is really a client-side check.`,
  why:[`A game client is in the attacker’s hands. Any rule enforced only in the client (currency, health, cooldowns, purchase state) is a suggestion.`,`Generated code reads well, so it gets less scrutiny than code that looks messy. Perry et al. found people were more confident in insecure answers with an assistant.`,`Faster output means more code per reviewer-hour. Security review does not shrink to match.`,`The costs are asymmetric: a cheating exploit or a leaked key hurts every player and the store account, while the feature saved an afternoon.`],
  think:{ q:[`Where does data enter here from outside the process: file, socket, store, deep link, mod, clipboard? What does the code assume about its size, shape and range?`,`Is any decision (grant, spend, rank, unlock) made only on the client’s word?`,`Does a purchase get verified with the store by the server, or does the client tell the game it paid?`,`Is there any secret, token or key in the source, config or shipped assets?`,`Did the change add a dependency or a call to a package name I do not recognise?`,`Were tests, limits or checks removed or loosened in the diff?`],
    trade:[`Server-side checks cost latency and backend work. Solo or offline games may accept client trust for single-player state and still need to validate files.`,`Strict validation rejects some legitimate old or modded saves; decide whether to migrate, repair or refuse.`,`Anti-cheat and obfuscation raise the cost of cheating and never reach zero; put the effort into what the server can decide.`,`Deep security review of every diff does not scale; concentrate on the parts that touch input, money, identity and secrets, and automate the rest (dependency scanning, secret scanning, static analysis).`],
    traps:[`Accepting code because the assistant said it was secure. The user study shows people rate insecure answers as secure more often with an assistant.`,`Validating on the client and trusting the result on the server.`,`Checking that a save parses, not that the values are in range, so a hand-edited count of 999999 or a negative price passes.`,`Trusting a "purchased" flag from the client or a receipt checked only locally.`,`Adding a package because the assistant named it. Check that it exists, is the one you mean and is maintained. (Rule of thumb; no study was found to cite for how often assistants invent names.)`,`Leaving API keys for an AI service in the client build; anyone can extract them.`],
    good:[`Every place outside data enters is listed, with its size limit, allowed values and failure behaviour.`,`The server, not the client, decides economy, rewards and rank.`,`Secret scanning and dependency checks run in CI and block the merge.`],
    bad:[`The review says only "looks fine" on code that parses input.`,`A security fix is one long prompt with no test that fails before the fix.`] },
  how:[`Read the diff starting with the boundaries. List every place the change reads from a file, socket, store, plugin or user, and every place it writes to a shared or persistent store.`,`For each input: set a maximum size; parse into a typed structure; reject unknown fields and values outside allowed ranges; on failure use a safe default or refuse, and log it.`,`Move authority to the server for anything that affects other players or money: currency changes, rewards, matchmaking, rank. The client sends intents ("I tried to buy item 12"); the server checks and answers.`,`Receipts: the server sends the receipt to the store’s verification service or uses the store’s server API, checks that the product, the user and the transaction id are right and not already redeemed, then grants the item. The client only displays the result.`,`Secrets: keep AI-service and backend keys on your server; the client gets short-lived tokens. Run secret scanning on every change.`,`Dependencies: for each new package, confirm the name, the owner and the licence, pin the version, and read what it does at install or import time.`,`Anti-cheat: treat client checks as friction. Log impossible values on the server (speed, score, timing) and act on the logs.`,`Ask the assistant for a review only as a second reader. Give it the specific threat ("what could a hand-edited save do here?") and confirm each finding by hand or with a test.`,`Write a failing test for each fixed hole: a hostile file, an out-of-range count, a replayed receipt.`],
  ai:{ yes:[`Generate hostile inputs for a parser: truncated, oversized, negative, wrong types, duplicate keys.`,`List every input boundary in a diff and the assumptions made about it.`,`Draft a threat list for a feature to check by hand.`],
       no:[`Certify code as secure.`,`Decide what the server should trust.`,`Choose or add packages without a person checking them.`] },
  prompts:[{l:'Boundary list',p:`Read this diff [DIFF]. List every place data enters the program from outside (file, network, store, mod, user input) and, for each one, what the code assumes about size, type and range, and what happens if the assumption is false. Do not say the code is secure; give me the list to check.`},
    {l:'Hostile inputs',p:`Here is the loader [CODE] and the format [SPEC]. Write ten hostile inputs (truncated, oversized, negative counts, wrong types, duplicate keys, unknown ids) and the behaviour I should expect for each. Output as test cases I can run.`}],
  verify:[`Is every outside input bounded, typed and range-checked?`,`Does the server decide anything that affects money, rewards or other players?`,`Does the server verify each purchase with the store?`,`Are there keys or tokens in source, config or shipped assets?`,`Was any new dependency confirmed by a person?`],
  test:[`Hand-edit a save (huge counts, negative values, wrong types, truncated) and confirm the game rejects or clamps it without crashing.`,`Replay a store receipt a second time and confirm nothing is granted.`,`Run a secret scanner on the repository and the built package.`],
  facts:[{claim:`Pearce et al., "Asleep at the Keyboard?" (August 2021): 89 scenarios based on the MITRE top-25 weaknesses, 1,689 programs generated by Copilot; about 40% contained vulnerabilities. Old model; not a rate for current tools.`,asOf:'2026-09-30',src:'https://arxiv.org/abs/2108.09293'},
    {claim:`Perry, Srivastava, Kumar, Boneh, "Do Users Write More Insecure Code with AI Assistants?" (November 2022, v3 December 2023): 47 participants (33 with an assistant, 14 control, 66% undergraduates), five security tasks, Codex; the assistant group was less secure on most tasks (for example 3% against 21% secure for message signing, 12% against 29% for file access; on the SQL task 36% of the assistant group wrote injectable code against 7% of the control group) and more likely to rate insecure answers as secure. Two-hour lab study with an old model.`,asOf:'2026-09-30',src:'https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.03622'},
    {claim:`Veracode, "2025 GenAI Code Security Report" (a security vendor’s own test, vendor claim): more than 100 models on Java, JavaScript, Python and C#; 45% of samples failed security tests (Java 72%, C# 45%, JavaScript 43%, Python 38%); cross-site scripting defended in only 14% of relevant samples; newer and larger models did not improve security. Task count and prompt design not stated on the pages read.`,asOf:'2026-09-30',src:'https://www.veracode.com/blog/genai-code-security-report/'}],
  rel:[['models-security-and-operations','That topic covers attacks through a model’s inputs; this one covers the code a model writes.'],['craft-save-systems','Loading a save is the first boundary to harden.'],['craft-over-defensive-code','Validate at the boundary and nowhere else; the two topics set where checks belong.'],['lead-code-review','Put boundaries and test changes first in a review of AI-written changes.'],['craft-verification-as-the-job','A hostile-input test is the check that proves a fix.']] });

ENGINE('craft-security-review-of-generated-code',{
  godot:{ term:`Treat a file as hostile. Read it with a size cap, parse into a Variant, check each type, and copy only allowed ids with clamped values into your own structure.`,
    api:['FileAccess.open() / get_length() / get_as_text()','JSON.parse_string()','typeof() with TYPE_DICTIONARY, TYPE_INT, TYPE_FLOAT','clampi()','Dictionary.has() / get()'],
    snippet:`const MAX_BYTES := 1_000_000
const MAX_STACK := {"potion": 99, "key": 1}   # allowed ids and limits

func load_inventory(path: String) -> Dictionary:
	var f := FileAccess.open(path, FileAccess.READ)
	if f == null or f.get_length() > MAX_BYTES:
		return {}
	var data = JSON.parse_string(f.get_as_text())
	if typeof(data) != TYPE_DICTIONARY or typeof(data.get("items")) != TYPE_DICTIONARY:
		return {}
	var out := {}
	for id in data["items"]:
		var n = data["items"][id]
		if MAX_STACK.has(id) and (typeof(n) == TYPE_INT or typeof(n) == TYPE_FLOAT):
			out[id] = clampi(int(n), 0, MAX_STACK[id])
	return out`,
    pitfall:`JSON.parse_string returns numbers as floats and returns null on bad input without an error you can see. Checking only for TYPE_INT rejects valid saves; skipping the type check lets an array or null reach int(), which raises an error, and lets a string like "5" pass as a count.`,
    map:`Unity’s JsonUtility gives you a typed object, so the type checks move to the class fields. Clamping and the allow-list are the same.` },
  unity:{ term:`Read with a size cap, deserialize into a plain data class, then copy only known ids with clamped counts into your own dictionary. Never use the deserialized object directly.`,
    api:['System.IO.FileInfo.Length','File.ReadAllText','JsonUtility.FromJson<T>','Mathf.Clamp','Dictionary.TryGetValue'],
    snippet:`using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.IO;
using UnityEngine;

[System.Serializable] public class ItemStack { public string id; public int count; }
[System.Serializable] public class InventorySave { public List<ItemStack> items; }

public static class SaveValidator {
    const long MaxBytes = 1_000_000;
    static readonly Dictionary<string, int> MaxStack = new() { { "potion", 99 }, { "key", 1 } };

    public static Dictionary<string, int> Load(string path) {
        var result = new Dictionary<string, int>();
        var info = new FileInfo(path);
        if (!info.Exists || info.Length > MaxBytes) return result;
        InventorySave save;
        try { save = JsonUtility.FromJson<InventorySave>(File.ReadAllText(path)); }
        catch (System.ArgumentException) { return result; }
        if (save?.items == null) return result;
        foreach (var s in save.items)
            if (s?.id != null && MaxStack.TryGetValue(s.id, out int max))
                result[s.id] = Mathf.Clamp(s.count, 0, max);
        return result;
    }
}`,
    pitfall:`JsonUtility throws ArgumentException on malformed JSON and fills missing fields with defaults, so an absent count looks like 0. Catch the exception, and do not treat "field missing" and "field is zero" as different facts.`,
    map:`Godot’s parse gives untyped values you must check yourself; here the class does the typing and you still allow-list ids and clamp counts.` } });

INTERVIEW('craft-security-review-of-generated-code',{
  junior:[
    { q:`Why can you not trust a check that only runs in the game client?`,
      a:`The player controls the machine. Memory, files and network calls can all be edited, so a client check is a hint. Anything that matters, such as currency or purchases, must be decided by a server.`,
      follow:`What does the client send instead?`,
      red:`Says obfuscation makes client checks safe.` }],
  mid:[
    { q:`An assistant wrote a save loader. What do you check before merging?`,
      a:`File size limit, parse into a typed structure, reject unknown fields, allowed ids, range checks on every number, and a safe result on failure. Then hostile-input tests: truncated, oversized, negative, wrong type. I would not rely on the assistant’s claim that it is safe.`,
      follow:`What about a save that mods legitimately extend?`,
      red:`Tests with one valid file only.` },
    { q:`What does the research say about AI-written code and security, and how much weight do you put on it?`,
      a:`Older studies (Pearce 2021 about 40% vulnerable programs; Perry 2022 lower security and more confidence with an assistant) and a vendor report (Veracode 2025, 45% of samples failing) all point one way. The models are old or the source has a commercial interest, so I treat it as a reason to review carefully, not as a current failure rate.`,
      follow:`How would you get your own number?`,
      red:`Quotes 45% as fact about today’s tools.` },
    { q:`You find a hard-coded API key in an AI-written change that is already merged. What do you do?`,
      a:`Treat the key as leaked: revoke and rotate it first, because removing it from the file does not remove it from history or builds. Then move the key to the server, add secret scanning to CI so it blocks the merge, and check usage logs for misuse.`,
      follow:`Why is deleting the line not enough?`,
      red:`Deletes the line and moves on.` }],
  senior:[
    { q:`A reviewer approves an AI-written network handler because the tests pass. What is missing?`,
      a:`Tests pass on the inputs the author thought of. I would ask for hostile inputs: oversized packets, wrong lengths, out-of-range values, replays and out-of-order messages, and ask what the server does when the client lies. Passing tests do not cover the abuse cases unless someone wrote them.`,
      follow:`Who should write the abuse tests?`,
      red:`Says green tests mean it is safe.` },
    { q:`Design store purchase handling for a mobile game so a modified client cannot grant itself items.`,
      a:`The client starts the purchase and sends the receipt to our server. The server verifies it with the store, checks product, user and transaction id, refuses replays, records it and grants the item. The client only shows the result. Add reconciliation for refunds and a log to spot impossible patterns.`,
      follow:`What if the server is down at purchase time?`,
      red:`Grants the item on the client when the store dialog closes.` }
  ] });

T('craft-ai-cost-latency-budget',{ d:'craft', t:'Cost and latency budget for AI features', tag:'Work out cost per request, per player and per day, and the slowest response a player will wait for, before you ship. Prices change; write down the date.',
  what:`A one-page budget for any feature that calls a language model at runtime, or any pipeline where agents work in your build. It has two halves. Cost: tokens in, tokens out, the price per token of the chosen model, the share of input served from cache, the number of calls per player per day, and a cap. Latency: how long the player waits for the first useful output and for the whole answer at the slow end (p95), and what the game shows meanwhile. Token prices are set by vendors and change often, so the numbers below are dated. On Anthropic’s public pricing page as read on 30 September 2026: Claude Sonnet 5.5 costs $2 per million input tokens and $10 per million output; Haiku 4.5 costs $1 and $5; Opus 5.5 costs $4 and $20; the top-priced Fable 5.1 costs $10 and $50. Prompt caching multiplies the base input price by 1.25 for a 5-minute write and 2 for a 1-hour write, and by 0.1 for a read (0.05 on Opus 5.5 and 0.025 on Fable 5.1). The Batch API halves both input and output prices for asynchronous work. Claude 4.7 and later models use a tokenizer that produces about 30% more tokens for the same text, so a budget made with an older model’s token counts is off. Sonnet 5’s introductory price of $2 and $10 was made permanent, and the increase to $3 and $15 planned for 1 September 2026 was cancelled: a reminder that prices move both ways. Fast mode, a research-preview option for some Opus models, costs a premium for lower latency. Other vendors have their own tables; check each. Capability also moves: METR’s time-horizon measurements say the length of task a model completes keeps growing, and its page says the current suite cannot measure reliably above 16 hours, so a cheaper model this year may do what a dearer one did last year. None of this is a tested method. It is arithmetic plus a habit of writing down assumptions and a date to check them.`,
  why:[`Cost scales with players, and a feature that costs a cent per session costs a fortune at ten million sessions. Without a per-player cap, one loop or one abusive player is an uncapped bill.`,`Latency is a design property. A character reply that takes eight seconds breaks a conversation that a one-second reply would carry.`,`Prices, tokenizers and models change on the vendor’s schedule, not yours. A budget with no dates goes stale silently.`,`In AI-assisted pipelines the spend is real too: agent sessions with long contexts and many tool calls add up, and the bill arrives at month end.`],
  think:{ q:[`What is the cost of one request, split into uncached input, cached input, cache write and output?`,`How many requests per player per day at the median, and at the 95th percentile? What is the hard cap?`,`What stays identical at the front of every prompt, so it can be cached?`,`Does the player need this answer live, or could it be generated in advance or in batch at half price?`,`What is the slowest acceptable time to first output, and what does the game show while it waits?`,`What happens when the cap is hit or the service is down: cached line, a hand-written fallback, or the feature turns off?`,`When will you re-check prices and the model choice?`],
    trade:[`A smaller model is cheaper and faster and may fail more on your eval set. Choose by measured pass rate on your cases, not by name.`,`Caching cuts cost and start-up time and pushes you to keep the front of the prompt frozen, which makes prompts harder to change.`,`Batch halves the price and takes you out of interactive use.`,`A hard cap protects the budget and can end a player’s experience mid-scene; decide the fallback first.`,`Pre-generating content is predictable in cost and gives up personalisation.`],
    traps:[`Budgeting on the list price of input only. Output tokens cost several times more per token (five times for the models listed above) and a chatty answer dominates the bill.`,`Assuming a cache hit rate you never measured. The 5-minute cache only helps if the same prefix is sent again within 5 minutes.`,`Using token counts from one model for another; the tokenizer change means the same text costs about 30% more tokens on Claude 4.7 and later.`,`Enforcing the cap only in the client. A modified client ignores it; keep the counter on the server.`,`Measuring latency on the developer’s fast connection. Measure on a phone on mobile data, at the 95th percentile.`,`Forgetting retries: a retry after timeout bills again.`],
    good:[`A budget sheet with dated prices, tokens per request, hit rate, calls per player, cap and p95 latency, all measured or labelled as assumed.`,`A fallback for every case where the model is unavailable or over budget.`,`A calendar date to re-check.`],
    bad:[`"About a cent a call" with no token counts.`,`The first sign of cost is the invoice.`] },
  how:[`Write the request shape: the stable prefix (rules, character sheet, tool list) and the variable part (player input, recent history), in tokens. Count with the vendor’s token-counting tool for the model you will ship, not a rule of thumb.`,`Set the maximum output tokens and the target latency. Log both from day one.`,`Compute cost per request: uncached input x input price + cached input x cache-read price + output x output price, plus a cache write on the first request of each cache period.`,`Estimate cache hit rate from real traffic: how often the same prefix is sent within the cache lifetime. Do not guess; instrument it.`,`Multiply by requests per player per day at the median and at the 95th percentile, then by daily active players. Compare with revenue per player.`,`Set caps at three levels: per request (max output), per player per day (server side), and per day for the whole feature (a circuit breaker that switches to the fallback).`,`Measure latency at the slow end: time to first token and total, on target devices and networks. Show something at once (an animation, a typing indicator, a stock line) and stream text where the feature allows.`,`Route by task: a small model for classification and short replies, a larger one for rare hard cases, batch for nightly content.`,`Put the sheet in the repository with the date, and add a recurring check to re-read the vendor pages and re-run your eval set on a cheaper option.`],
  ai:{ yes:[`Build the spreadsheet of the budget from your inputs and check formulas.`,`Propose cache-friendly prompt structures where the stable part comes first.`,`Draft the fallback lines for capped or failed calls.`],
       no:[`State current prices from memory. Copy them from the vendor’s page with the date.`,`Enforce the cap. The server does.`] },
  prompts:[{l:'Budget sheet',p:`Build a budget for the feature [FEATURE]. Inputs I give you: stable prefix [TOKENS] tokens, variable input [TOKENS], output [TOKENS], model prices [PRICES with date], cache read multiplier [X], hit rate [Y or unknown], requests per player per day (median and p95) [N], daily players [D]. Give cost per request, per player per day, per day, and the effect of hit rate at 0%, 50% and 90%. Show the formulas.`},
    {l:'Fallback plan',p:`The feature [FEATURE] calls a model in real time. List the cases where it will be over the cap, slow (more than [T] seconds) or unavailable, and propose a player-facing fallback for each that keeps the game playable.`}],
  verify:[`Are prices copied from the vendor page with a date, for the model you will ship?`,`Were tokens counted with that model’s counter?`,`Is the cache hit rate measured, or marked as an assumption?`,`Is the cap enforced on the server, with a fallback?`,`Was p95 latency measured on target hardware and network?`],
  test:[`Replay a day of logged requests against the sheet and compare predicted cost with the vendor’s usage report; a gap over 10% means an assumption is wrong.`,`Force the cap: send requests past it and confirm the fallback appears and no more calls are billed.`,`Throttle the network and check the loading state, the timeout and that a retry does not bill twice for one player action.`],
  facts:[{claim:`Anthropic pricing page, read 30 September 2026: Claude Sonnet 5.5 $2 per million input tokens and $10 output; Haiku 4.5 $1 and $5; Opus 5.5 $4 and $20; Fable 5.1 $10 and $50. Cache multipliers on base input: 5-minute write 1.25x, 1-hour write 2x, read 0.1x (0.05x on Opus 5.5, 0.025x on Fable 5.1). Vendor prices; check the page again before relying on them.`,asOf:'2026-09-30',src:'https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/about-claude/pricing'},
    {claim:`Anthropic pricing page, 30 September 2026: the Batch API gives a 50% discount on input and output tokens for asynchronous requests; Claude 4.7 and later models use a tokenizer that produces about 30% more tokens for the same text; Sonnet 5’s $2/$10 launch price became the standard price and the planned rise to $3/$15 on 1 September 2026 was cancelled.`,asOf:'2026-09-30',src:'https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/about-claude/pricing'},
    {claim:`METR time-horizons page (updated 8 May 2026): the exponential trend fits better than linear or hyperbolic, and measurements above 16 hours are unreliable with the current task suite; tasks are self-contained and well specified, so this is not a measure of autonomy on messy real work.`,asOf:'2026-09-30',src:'https://metr.org/time-horizons/'}],
  rel:[['models-prompt-caching','The mechanics of the cache that the input side of the budget relies on.'],['models-security-and-operations','Caps, replay costs and abuse limits belong in the same sheet.'],['ai-budgets-and-debugging','The CPU and debugging cost of game AI; this topic covers model-call spend and wait.'],['models-open-and-closed','Running a model yourself changes the cost from per token to per server.'],['craft-measuring-ai-uplift','The same discipline of measuring instead of assuming, applied to team output.']] });

ENGINE('craft-ai-cost-latency-budget',{
  godot:{ term:`A small object that tracks estimated spend against a daily cap and says yes or no before a call. It is a client-side estimator for pacing and demos; the enforcing counter belongs on your server.`,
    api:['RefCounted / class_name','float arithmetic with int * float','Time.get_ticks_msec() to time a call (not in the snippet)'],
    snippet:`class_name AiBudget
extends RefCounted

var price_in := 2.0 / 1_000_000.0    # USD per token; copy from the vendor page, note the date
var price_out := 10.0 / 1_000_000.0
var day_cap_usd := 0.05
var spent_usd := 0.0

func can_call(est_in: int, max_out: int) -> bool:
	return spent_usd + est_in * price_in + max_out * price_out <= day_cap_usd

func record(used_in: int, used_out: int) -> void:
	spent_usd += used_in * price_in + used_out * price_out`,
    pitfall:`Estimating with max_out is deliberately pessimistic; using the average lets a burst of long answers pass the check. Also the counter resets with the process, so a restart clears a client-side cap.`,
    map:`Same logic in a C# class in Unity; both keep the real counter on the server.` },
  unity:{ term:`A plain C# class that checks an estimated worst-case cost against the day’s cap before a request and records actual usage from the response afterwards.`,
    api:['double arithmetic','System.Diagnostics.Stopwatch to time a call (not in the snippet)'],
    snippet:`public class AiBudget {
    const double PriceIn = 2.0 / 1_000_000;    // USD per token; copy from the vendor page, note the date
    const double PriceOut = 10.0 / 1_000_000;
    readonly double dayCapUsd;
    double spentUsd;

    public AiBudget(double dayCapUsd) { this.dayCapUsd = dayCapUsd; }

    public bool CanCall(int estIn, int maxOut) =>
        spentUsd + estIn * PriceIn + maxOut * PriceOut <= dayCapUsd;

    public void Record(int usedIn, int usedOut) =>
        spentUsd += usedIn * PriceIn + usedOut * PriceOut;
}`,
    pitfall:`Tokens billed come from the response’s usage numbers, not from your estimate; record those. And a client-side counter is bypassable, so treat it as pacing, and enforce on the server.`,
    map:`Godot version is the same class in GDScript.` } });

INTERVIEW('craft-ai-cost-latency-budget',{
  junior:[
    { q:`What two costs make up one call to a language model?`,
      a:`Input tokens (what you send) and output tokens (what it writes). Output tokens usually cost several times more per token, so a long answer costs more than a long question.`,
      follow:`What makes an input token cheaper?`,
      red:`Says the price is per request.` },
    { q:`Why write the date next to a price in a budget?`,
      a:`Vendors change prices, tokenizers and models. A budget with no date cannot be told from a stale one. The date says when to check again.`,
      follow:`What else can change the cost of the same text?`,
      red:`Says prices are stable.` }],
  mid:[
    { q:`Your NPC dialogue feature costs $0.008 a call and players average 20 calls a day. Is it affordable?`,
      a:`That is $0.16 per player per day, so it depends on revenue per player and on whether all players use it. I would check the token counts, see how much of the prompt can be cached, compare a smaller model on the eval set, set a per-player cap and see what the game does at the cap.`,
      follow:`How would caching change it?`,
      red:`Answers yes or no without asking about revenue per player.` },
    { q:`A feature is fast on your desk and slow for players. What do you look at?`,
      a:`The 95th percentile time to first output on real devices and mobile networks, not the average on office wifi. Then output length, model choice, retries and what the screen shows while waiting. Streaming and a loading state change how long it feels even when total time is the same.`,
      follow:`What would you cap?`,
      red:`Quotes an average measured locally.` },
    { q:`A model you rely on is replaced by one with a different tokenizer. What do you re-check?`,
      a:`Token counts for the same prompts, since the same text can cost about 30% more tokens on newer Claude tokenizers, then price per token, cache behaviour and latency, and the pass rate on my eval set. I update the dated budget sheet and the caps.`,
      follow:`How do you count tokens for the new model?`,
      red:`Reuses old token counts.` }],
  senior:[
    { q:`Design the budget and rollout for an AI companion feature to a game with a million daily players.`,
      a:`Cost per request from measured tokens and dated prices, split by cache hit. Calls per player at the median and the 95th percentile. Caps per request, per player and per day with a circuit breaker on the server. Latency target and a loading state. Route by task and use batch for anything not live. Roll out to a percentage, watch cost and quality against the sheet, and put a date on the price and model re-check.`,
      follow:`Prices drop 50% next quarter. What changes?`,
      red:`Gives a single per-call cost with no cap, fallback or measurement.` }
  ] });

T('craft-cpu-cache-and-data-layout',{ d:'craft', t:'CPU time, caches and data layout', tag:'The CPU is rarely slow at arithmetic. It waits for memory. Lay data out so each fetch brings in only what the loop needs, then spread, skip or parallelise the work that remains.',
  what:`A CPU core runs in the low billions of cycles a second, and main memory answers in tens to a hundred or so nanoseconds, which is hundreds of cycles. Caches hide the gap. Memory moves in cache lines, 64 bytes on mainstream x86 and many Arm cores (Drepper notes the norm moved from 32 to 64). A load that hits level 1 costs a few cycles, level 2 around ten to fifteen, level 3 forty to seventy or more, and a miss to RAM several hundred. Those are orders of magnitude, not promises: they vary by chip, clock and load, so measure on your target. The practical result is that a loop is limited by how many cache lines it drags in, not by how many additions it does. This topic is the set of levers that follow. Data layout decides how many lines a loop touches: structure of arrays instead of array of structs, hot fields apart from cold ones, no pointer chasing. Branch prediction and SIMD decide how well the core uses each line once it has it. Algorithms and scheduling decide how much work exists at all: a grid instead of all pairs, a dirty flag instead of recomputing, a slice of the agents each frame. Threads then multiply what is left, and bring false sharing and synchronisation as their own bill. Mike Acton’s CppCon 2014 talk “Data-Oriented Design and C++” is the usual starting point for the whole mindset; as commonly summarised, it argues that code exists to transform data, so design from the data and the hardware. Nystrom’s Game Programming Patterns chapter on data locality gives the game-sized version, including hot and cold splitting. Everything here is a lens to test against a capture, not a law: a loop that runs 200 times a frame does not need any of it.`,
  why:[`A cache miss to RAM can cost as much as hundreds of arithmetic instructions, so the layout of a hot loop often matters more than the cleverness of its maths.`,`Phones have smaller caches, slower memory and thermal limits. A scene that holds 60 fps on a desktop can miss budget on a phone for exactly this reason (see craft-performance for the budget method).`,`Most O(n squared) disasters are invisible at 200 units and fatal at 5,000. Counting pair checks before building is cheap; discovering them in a profile the week before launch is not.`,`Threads are the tempting fix and the wrong first one. A frame that is bound by cache misses on one core gets little from eight cores that fetch the same scattered data, and false sharing can make it slower.`,`Frame time spikes, not averages, are what players feel. Work that can be sliced, cached or pooled turns a 40 ms spike into eight 5 ms steps.`],
  think:{ q:[`Which loop is hot? Give it as a share of the frame from a capture on the target device, not a guess.`,`How many bytes does that loop read per element, and how many of them does it actually use? A 128-byte object read for 16 bytes wastes seven eighths of the object, and three quarters of each 64-byte line fetched.`,`Does the loop follow pointers (list of objects, each holding a reference to a component) or walk an array?`,`Is the cost the number of elements, the number of pairs, or the number of times a result is recomputed unchanged?`,`Is the data read-only in this loop? Read-only data can be shared across threads freely; written data needs ownership or a join.`,`Does every entity need this every frame? Could far, hidden or idle ones update at a lower rate?`,`If you add a thread, which cache lines will two cores write? That is the false-sharing question.`],
    trade:[`Structure of arrays makes one loop fast and a different access pattern (read everything about one entity) slower and more awkward to write. Split by loop, not by principle.`,`A grid is trivial and fast for evenly spread things of similar size. A quadtree or BVH handles clustering and mixed sizes and costs build and update work. Nystrom’s pattern chapter lists the same trade.`,`Time slicing flattens spikes and adds latency: with ten slices a decision can be ten frames old. Fine for perception, not for a hit test.`,`Caching with a dirty flag saves recomputation and adds a place where a missed invalidation shows a stale value, which is a correctness bug rather than a slow frame.`,`Pooling removes allocation and instantiation spikes, and costs resident memory, reset code and a class of stale-state bugs.`,`Jobs and Burst give large speedups for bulk numeric work and constrain you to unmanaged data and no engine object access inside the job.`],
    traps:[`Rewriting layout before profiling, then discovering the frame was GPU bound or dominated by one script call.`,`Splitting data into arrays and then looping with a lookup per element (an index into a dictionary), so the cache win is spent on hash lookups.`,`Adding threads to a loop that is memory bound, or to work so small that scheduling costs more than the work. Godot’s own documentation warns that the thread pool can hurt when tasks are not computationally expensive.`,`Two threads writing neighbouring counters in one array, so each write invalidates the other core’s copy of the line.`,`Pooling plain value objects or small allocations the runtime already handles cheaply, and paying the reset bugs for nothing.`,`A grid with a cell size much smaller than the objects, so each object lands in dozens of cells; or much larger, so a cell holds half the world and the grid degenerates to all pairs.`,`Trusting a Deep Profile or editor capture for micro-costs. Unity’s manual says Deep Profiling makes the application run significantly slower, and instrumenting every call weighs most on small functions (our inference), so treat its numbers as relative.`,`Benchmarking one run on a cold cache, then one on a warm cache, and comparing them.`],
    good:[`The team can say, for the three hottest loops, how many bytes each element costs and how many of them the loop uses.`,`Neighbour queries go through a grid or tree, with the cell size chosen from the object radius and written next to the code.`,`Expensive per-agent work is sliced round-robin with a stated worst-case latency.`,`Every optimisation lands with a before and after capture from a build on the target device.`],
    bad:[`"Make it multithreaded" is the plan, with no capture showing where the single thread spends time.`,`Every enemy has an Update method that scans all other enemies.`,`A cache that is never invalidated, or invalidated everywhere, so it either lies or does nothing.`] },
  how:[`Capture first. Take a development build on the target phone or console, play the worst scene, and find the top three costs on the main thread and the worker threads. Note frame time percentiles, not one frame. Use the engine’s own tool first (Unity Profiler, with Profile Analyzer to compare two sets of frames; Unreal Insights; the Godot debugger Profiler), and a native sampling profiler such as Superluminal or Tracy when you need to see inside native or engine code.`,`Count before you change. For each hot loop write down: elements per frame, bytes per element, bytes read per element. For 5,000 enemies at 128 bytes each the loop strides over 640,000 bytes and touches 5,000 cache lines at best (one per enemy, if both fields sit in one line) and 10,000 at worst, with at most 16 of every 64 bytes fetched used. If it reads only 16 bytes of position and velocity (two float2 values), packing those into two arrays touches 80,000 bytes, 1,250 lines, and the working set drops from 640 KB to 80 KB. That is larger than an L1 cache and the L2 of many phone cores before, and about L1 size after; on desktop cores with 1 MB or more of L2 both versions fit in L2 and the gain is fewer lines fetched per frame and a stream the prefetcher can follow.`,`Split hot from cold. Keep what the per-frame loop reads (position, velocity, state, timers) in packed arrays. Move what is read rarely (loot tables, names, designer data) to a separate array indexed by the same id, or to the object itself.`,`Remove pointer chasing from the hot path: store plain values in arrays of structs or arrays of numbers, and refer to other entities by integer index, not by object reference.`,`Keep arrays dense. Swap-remove on death (move the last element into the freed slot) so the loop has no holes and no per-element "is it alive" branch, as Nystrom’s particle example does.`,`Fix the O(n squared) first, because no layout beats a smaller n. Put units in a uniform grid with a cell about as wide as the largest query radius, and test only the 3 by 3 neighbouring cells. For 2,000 units, all pairs is about 2 million tests a frame; for 10,000 it is about 50 million, which at even one nanosecond each is 50 ms.`,`Slice the rest. Give each expensive per-agent job (perception, path request, line-of-sight) a round-robin slice: with 1,000 agents and a 0.05 ms job, doing all of them costs 50 ms; doing 100 a frame costs 5 ms and each agent refreshes every ten frames, about 167 ms at 60 Hz. Choose the slice count from the latency the behaviour can stand.`,`Cache with a dirty flag where a result is read often and changes rarely (world transforms, derived stats, navigation costs). Set the flag at the single place the inputs change, recompute on first read, and test the invalidation path.`,`Remove branches from hot loops where you can: sort or bucket items by type so a loop handles one kind, replace unpredictable if-else on data with arithmetic or a lookup table, and keep rare cases in a separate pass. A mispredicted branch costs on the order of fifteen to twenty cycles on recent x86 cores. SIMD helps the same loops: one instruction works on 4, 8 or 16 values at once, which needs the values to sit next to each other and share a type, so packed arrays feed SIMD loads directly (Burst does this for you when the loop allows it).`,`Then, and only then, add threads to bulk work that has no shared writes. Unity: an IJobParallelFor over a NativeArray with Burst. Unreal: UE::Tasks::Launch or a parallel-for over chunks. Godot: WorkerThreadPool.add_group_task over index ranges. Give each thread its own range or its own output, and join once.`,`Pad per-thread counters. If threads must write their own accumulator in a shared array, space the slots 64 bytes apart or give each thread a local and add them at the end, so no two threads write the same cache line.`,`Pool only what a capture shows costing: instantiated prefabs, particles with components, objects whose constructor allocates. Reset every field on release and check for double release in development builds.`,`Re-measure. Keep the before and after capture with the change, and add the scene to a benchmark so the gain is guarded.`],
  ai:{ yes:[`Count bytes per element and cache lines touched for a struct and a loop you paste, and propose a hot and cold split.`,`Rewrite an array-of-objects loop as parallel arrays with swap-remove and keep behaviour identical.`,`Sketch a uniform grid for neighbour queries and compute a cell size from the radius, with the insert, move and query code.`,`Turn a loop into an IJobParallelFor with Burst, or a WorkerThreadPool group task, and list the data it must not touch from a thread.`,`Read a profiler summary and list the top costs, then say which are layout, algorithm or scheduling problems.`],
       no:[`Say a layout change will give a speedup. It can predict fewer cache lines, never measure the frame time on your phone.`,`Know your hardware’s cache sizes or latencies. Quote those from the vendor or measure them.`,`Decide the slice count or pool size; those come from the latency a design can stand and from captures.`,`Guarantee a job is free of races. Review ownership of every written location and run the engine’s safety checks.`] },
  prompts:[{l:'Count the cache lines',p:`Here is a struct and the per-frame loop that uses it: [PASTE]. There are [N] elements and the target is [PLATFORM] with [64]-byte cache lines. Tell me bytes per element, bytes the loop actually reads, cache lines touched per frame as it is, and as a hot and cold split. Do not claim a time saving; give me the measurement I should take on the device to confirm it.`},
    {l:'Replace all pairs',p:`This [LANGUAGE] code compares every [ENTITY] with every other each frame: [PASTE]. There are about [N] at peak and the largest interaction radius is [R]. Replace it with a uniform grid. State the cell size and why, how an entity that moves between cells is updated, how a query reads the neighbouring cells, and what happens for an entity wider than one cell. Keep results identical to the all-pairs version and add a debug check that compares the two.`},
    {l:'Parallelise safely',p:`Here is a loop over [N] [ENTITIES]: [PASTE]. List every variable it reads and writes. For each write say whether two iterations can touch the same location or the same 64-byte cache line. Then write it as [an IJobParallelFor with Burst | a WorkerThreadPool group task | UE::Tasks] with chunking, say what must be copied or made read-only, and name the exact join point.`}],
  verify:[`Is there a capture from the target device, taken before and after, with the same scene and settings?`,`Does the result match the old code exactly? Run both paths in a debug build and compare outputs for a few thousand frames, especially after a grid or SoA rewrite.`,`Does the grid return the same neighbours as the all-pairs version, including entities on a cell boundary and entities wider than a cell?`,`Does the dirty-flag cache ever return a stale value? Change every input in a test and read the result.`,`Does a pooled object come back clean? Release one in a dirty state and reacquire it.`,`Does the threaded version still match the single-threaded one with the engine’s safety checks on? In Unity leave the job safety system on in the Editor.`,`Is the capture a normal development build, not Deep Profile?`],
  test:[`Run the benchmark scene at 1x, 2x and 4x the entity count and plot frame time. Linear growth is expected; a curve bending upward is an O(n squared) you missed.`,`Record frame time percentiles (50th, 95th, 99th) with slicing on and off. The 99th should fall; the average will barely move.`,`Test on the slowest device you support, after ten minutes of play, so thermal throttling and a smaller cache are in the result.`,`Test a clustered case on purpose (all units in one cell, or all near one corner) because a grid that works on evenly spread data may collapse.`,`Compare a single thread with the job version at several batch sizes. Too small and scheduling dominates; too large and one thread is left with the tail.`,`For false sharing, run the threaded loop with per-thread counters adjacent and then padded. If the padded run is faster, the line was shared.`] ,
  facts:[{claim:`Unity ECS stores entities of one archetype in 16 KiB chunks, laying each component type out as its own array inside the chunk.`,asOf:'2026-10-05',src:'https://docs.unity3d.com/Packages/com.unity.entities@1.3/manual/concepts-archetypes.html'},
    {claim:`Unity’s ObjectPool<T> takes createFunc, actionOnGet, actionOnRelease, actionOnDestroy, collectionCheck, defaultCapacity and maxSize; it stores items in a stack so the objects are not necessarily contiguous in memory, and it is not safe to call from background threads.`,asOf:'2026-10-05',src:'https://docs.unity3d.com/6000.0/Documentation/ScriptReference/Pool.ObjectPool_1.html'},
    {claim:`Burst compiles a subset of C# (High-Performance C#) with LLVM; branches inside a loop can stop it vectorising, and Loop.ExpectVectorized (behind UNITY_BURST_EXPERIMENTAL_LOOP_INTRINSICS) makes the compiler report when a loop is not vectorised.`,asOf:'2026-10-05',src:'https://docs.unity3d.com/Packages/com.unity.burst@1.8/manual/optimization-loop-vectorization.html'},
    {claim:`The Godot debugger Profiler does not currently support C# scripts; the documentation points to Rider or dotTrace with the Godot plugins instead.`,asOf:'2026-10-05',src:'https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/tutorials/scripting/debug/the_profiler.html'},
    {claim:`Godot’s WorkerThreadPool.add_group_task(action, elements, tasks_needed = -1, high_priority = false, description = "") runs a Callable once per element on pooled worker threads; the scene tree is not thread-safe.`,asOf:'2026-10-05',src:'https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class_workerthreadpool.html'},
    {claim:`Unreal’s Tasks System launches work with UE::Tasks::Launch, supports prerequisites, nested tasks and pipes, and shares its scheduler and worker threads with the older TaskGraph.`,asOf:'2026-10-05',src:'https://dev.epicgames.com/documentation/en-us/unreal-engine/tasks-systems-in-unreal-engine'},
    {claim:`Cache lines are 64 bytes on mainstream processors; Drepper quotes Intel’s figures for a Pentium M of about 3 cycles for L1d, 14 for L2 and 240 for main memory. Current chips are faster in absolute terms but the ratios are of the same order.`,asOf:'2026-10-05',src:'https://lwn.net/Articles/252125/'},
    {claim:`Unity’s Deep Profile setting instruments all function calls, is resource-intensive and makes the application run significantly slower; Unity exposes the cache line size as JobsUtility.CacheLineSize.`,asOf:'2026-10-05',src:'https://docs.unity3d.com/6000.0/Documentation/Manual/profiler-deep-profiling.html'}],
  rel:[['craft-performance','The budget and the CPU or GPU bound check come first; this topic is the CPU side of what that topic tells you to fix.'],['craft-entities-and-scenes','ECS stores components in packed per-type arrays, which is the structure-of-arrays idea built into the engine.'],['craft-memory-and-gc','Allocation rate and pooling meet here: a pooled object avoids both the allocator and the collector, and pooled objects are not contiguous.'],['craft-game-loop-timestep','Slicing work across frames and the cap on steps per frame are the loop-level controls for CPU spikes.'],['ai-budgets-and-debugging','AI tick rates and time slicing are this topic’s amortisation applied to agents, with the debugging side that topic adds.'],['server-world-partitioning','Grids and cells appear on the server as shards and zones; the client-side grid here is the same cut at a smaller scale.']] });
TECH('craft-cpu-cache-and-data-layout',[
  {n:'Structure of arrays with hot and cold split', how:'Store each hot field in its own packed array (x[], y[], vx[], vy[]) or group fields the loop reads together, and put rarely read data in a cold array indexed by the same id.', fit:'Thousands of similar entities and one or two loops that read a few fields of each. Particles, projectiles, crowds, bullets, simulation cells.', cost:'Code is less natural to read. Reading everything about one entity touches several arrays. Adding a field means editing parallel arrays.', alt:'Keep array of structs but shrink the struct (smaller types, indices instead of pointers); or ECS, which does the split for you.'},
  {n:'Swap-remove in a dense array', how:'On removal, copy the last live element into the freed slot and shrink the count. The array stays packed with no holes.', fit:'Entities that die often and are looped over every frame.', cost:'Order is not stable and indices held elsewhere go stale, so other code must hold ids that map to slots.', alt:'A free list with an alive flag, which keeps indices stable and puts a branch and wasted lines in the loop.'},
  {n:'Uniform grid or spatial hash', how:'Divide space into cells about as wide as the largest query radius, bucket entities by cell, and query the cell and its neighbours.', fit:'Many objects of similar size spread fairly evenly: units, bullets, crowd steering, collision broad phase.', cost:'Memory for cells, work to move entities between cells, and a bad fit when objects vary widely in size or cluster tightly.', alt:'A quadtree or octree for uneven density, a BVH for mostly static geometry, a k-d tree for nearest-neighbour. See Nystrom’s spatial partition chapter.'},
  {n:'Round-robin time slicing', how:'Keep an index; each frame process N agents from it, advance it, wrap. Or give a per-frame microsecond budget and stop when spent.', fit:'Work whose result may be a few frames old: perception, path requests, target choice, off-screen updates.', cost:'Added latency, and agents can be at different phases of decision, which complicates debugging. A budget-based slice makes cost steady and latency variable.', alt:'Lower tick rate by distance or importance (LOD for logic), or move the work to a worker thread.'},
  {n:'Dirty flag', how:'Mark a value dirty at the single place its inputs change; recompute lazily on the next read and clear the flag.', fit:'Derived data read often and changed rarely: world transforms, aggregate stats, navigation costs, UI layout.', cost:'A missed invalidation is a stale-value bug that shows up as wrong behaviour, not slowness. Chains of flags need an order.', alt:'Recompute every frame when it is cheap, or version numbers that consumers compare.'},
  {n:'Object pool', how:'Create objects up front, hand them out, reset and return them instead of destroying. In Unity use UnityEngine.Pool.ObjectPool<T>.', fit:'Instantiating or allocating costs a measured spike: prefab instances with components, particles, projectiles, UI rows. Pairs with the GC discipline in craft-memory-and-gc.', cost:'Resident memory, reset code, stale-state bugs. Pooled objects are not contiguous, so pooling does not fix cache layout. Not for cheap structs.', alt:'Arrays of plain values reused in place; a ring buffer; no pool if the runtime allocator is not in the capture.'},
  {n:'Bucket by type to cut branch misses', how:'Sort or partition items so each loop handles one kind, or turn a data-dependent branch into arithmetic or a table lookup.', fit:'A hot loop with an unpredictable branch on data, such as a type or state switch per element.', cost:'Extra pass to bucket, and the gain disappears if the branch was already predictable. Measure branch misses with a hardware-counter tool (Intel VTune, AMD uProf or Linux perf), because the engine profilers do not report them; do not judge by eye.', alt:'Leave it. Modern predictors do well on regular patterns and a branch seen as costly in source may not be.'},
  {n:'Jobs with Burst, or a worker-thread pool', how:'Split bulk work into chunks that write to disjoint ranges, schedule on worker threads, join once. Unity: IJobParallelFor with [BurstCompile]. Unreal: UE::Tasks. Godot: WorkerThreadPool.add_group_task.', fit:'Bulk numeric work with no shared writes and enough work per chunk to cover scheduling cost: integration, steering, culling, skinning prep.', cost:'Restrictions on data (Unity jobs use unmanaged data; Godot scene tree is not thread-safe), a join point that can stall the main thread, and false sharing or races if ownership is loose.', alt:'Fix layout and algorithm first. Often a packed single-thread loop is enough and far easier to debug.'},
  {n:'Pad or localise per-thread writes', how:'Give each thread a local accumulator and merge at the end, or space shared slots 64 bytes apart.', fit:'Threads that update counters, bounds or lists in a shared structure.', cost:'Memory and a merge step. C++17 exposes the idea as std::hardware_destructive_interference_size and Unity exposes JobsUtility.CacheLineSize; elsewhere use 64 and check the target.', alt:'Write results to per-chunk output arrays and read them after the join.'}]);
ENGINE('craft-cpu-cache-and-data-layout',{
  unity:{ term:`Unity’s answer is Jobs plus Burst plus unmanaged data. A job is a struct run on worker threads; Burst compiles it to SIMD-friendly native code. NativeArray holds the data in contiguous unmanaged memory, and the safety system catches many races in the Editor. ECS (Entities) goes further and stores components in 16 KiB chunks of per-type arrays.`,
    api:['Unity.Jobs.IJobParallelFor','Unity.Burst.BurstCompile','Unity.Collections.NativeArray<T>','Unity.Mathematics.float2','IJobParallelForExtensions.Schedule()','JobHandle.Complete()','Unity.Collections.ReadOnlyAttribute'],
    snippet:`using Unity.Burst;
using Unity.Collections;
using Unity.Jobs;
using Unity.Mathematics;
[BurstCompile]
struct Integrate : IJobParallelFor {
    public NativeArray<float2> pos;
    [ReadOnly] public NativeArray<float2> vel;
    public float dt;
    public void Execute(int i) { pos[i] += vel[i] * dt; }
}
// pos, vel: Allocator.Persistent; Dispose() them when done.
// Update(): 64 iterations per steal; Complete() joins here.
// new Integrate { pos = pos, vel = vel, dt = Time.deltaTime }
//     .Schedule(pos.Length, 64).Complete();`,
    pitfall:`Calling Complete() straight after Schedule() removes the overlap: the main thread waits for the job. It is still a win for a big loop spread over cores, but schedule early in the frame and complete late (or next frame) to hide the cost. Also keep a branch out of the hot loop: Burst may stop vectorising it, which Loop.ExpectVectorized can check at compile time.`,
    map:`A Unity job is a Godot WorkerThreadPool group task over index ranges; NativeArray<float2> is a PackedVector2Array; the Burst Inspector has no Godot equivalent, so for GDScript the fix for a hot loop is usually typed packed arrays, a server, or a GDExtension.` },
  godot:{ term:`Godot gives you packed arrays (PackedFloat32Array, PackedVector2Array) for contiguous data and WorkerThreadPool for bulk parallel work. The debugger Profiler shows per-function self and inclusive time for GDScript; it does not currently cover C#. Most servers can be called from threads (rendering and physics need thread-safe operation enabled in project settings first); the scene tree cannot.`,
    api:['WorkerThreadPool.add_group_task()','WorkerThreadPool.wait_for_group_task_completion()','PackedFloat32Array','Callable.bind()','mini()','ceili()'],
    snippet:`extends Node
const CHUNK := 1024
var xs := PackedFloat32Array()

func _ready() -> void:
\txs.resize(100000)

func _process(dt: float) -> void:
\tvar n := ceili(xs.size() / float(CHUNK))
\tvar id := WorkerThreadPool.add_group_task(_step.bind(dt), n)
\tWorkerThreadPool.wait_for_group_task_completion(id)

func _step(c: int, dt: float) -> void:
\tfor i in range(c * CHUNK, mini((c + 1) * CHUNK, xs.size())):
\t\txs[i] += 60.0 * dt`,
    pitfall:`Each element is one call of the Callable. 100,000 calls of a tiny GDScript function cost more than the work, so the snippet makes each call handle 1,024 elements. Every group task must be waited on, and calling wait straight away (as the snippet does) removes any overlap with the main thread, the same point as the Unity pitfall. As a safe rule, no task touches nodes or the scene tree. GDScript’s per-element cost is also high, so for heavy numeric loops measure before trusting threads to rescue it.`,
    map:`A WorkerThreadPool group task is Unity’s IJobParallelFor with the chunk size as innerloopBatchCount; Godot has no Burst, so the per-element speed is the language’s.` },
  note:`Unreal sits between: UE::Tasks::Launch and its task graph give the thread pool, C++ gives you control of layout, and Unreal Insights is the capture tool. The order of work is the same in all three engines: capture, count bytes and pairs, fix layout and algorithm, then thread, then re-measure.` });
INTERVIEW('craft-cpu-cache-and-data-layout',{
  junior:[
    { q:`What is a cache line, and why does it matter for a loop over an array?`,
      a:`The unit memory moves in, typically 64 bytes. Reading one byte brings in its whole line, so a loop over contiguous data uses every byte it fetches, while one that jumps between scattered objects fetches a full line for a few bytes. Mention the latency gap: a few cycles for L1, hundreds for RAM, as orders of magnitude.`,
      follow:`How many lines does reading 1,000 consecutive 4-byte floats touch?`,
      red:`Thinks caching is about whether the variable is in a register, or cannot say what is wasted when you read part of a line.` },
    { q:`What does a profiler tell you that guessing does not, and what is one way it can mislead?`,
      a:`It shows where the frame time goes by function and thread. It can mislead when instrumentation distorts small functions (Deep Profile), when you capture the editor not the device, or when you read one frame instead of many.`,
      follow:`Which tool would you use on a phone build?`,
      red:`Optimises the function that looks slow in the source without a capture.` }
  ],
  mid:[
    { q:`Array of structs against structure of arrays: when does the second win, and what does it cost?`,
      a:`When a hot loop reads a few fields of many elements: SoA touches only those fields, so fewer cache lines, and SIMD can load several values at once. Cost: readability, and any code that wants all fields of one entity touches several arrays. Give the 5,000 by 128 bytes against 16 bytes example.`,
      follow:`What is hot and cold splitting, and where do you draw the line?`,
      red:`Says SoA is always faster, or does not mention the access pattern.` },
    { q:`A scene with 3,000 units runs at 12 fps and the profile shows 70 percent in a unit-versus-unit check. What do you do?`,
      a:`Count pairs: about 4.5 million per frame. Replace with a uniform grid (cell about the largest query radius, test the 3 by 3 neighbourhood), verify results match the old version, re-capture. Only if the grid loop is still hot consider layout or threads.`,
      follow:`When is a grid the wrong choice?`,
      red:`Jumps to threading the all-pairs loop.` },
    { q:`How would you spread an expensive per-agent job across frames, and what do you give up?`,
      a:`Round-robin slices, or a per-frame microsecond budget. You give up freshness: with N slices a result can be N frames old, so choose per behaviour. Debugging gets harder because agents are at different phases.`,
      follow:`How would you choose the slice size?`,
      red:`Reduces the frame rate of the whole game instead.` },
    { q:`What is a dirty flag and what is its failure mode?`,
      a:`A marker that a cached derived value is stale, set where inputs change and cleared on recompute. It fails when an input changes without setting the flag, which shows as a stale value, not as slowness.`,
      follow:`Where do you set it so that cannot happen?`,
      red:`Sets it in many call sites, or never tests the invalidation.` }
  ],
  senior:[
    { q:`A job-parallel loop is slower than the single-threaded one. Give five reasons and how you would tell them apart.`,
      a:`Chunks too small so scheduling dominates; memory bound loop so extra cores wait on the same bus; false sharing from adjacent per-thread writes; a join that stalls the main thread; the job reads managed data or does a lookup per element, so the layout gain is spent. Tell them apart with a thread timeline capture, varying batch size, padding the writes, and checking the loop’s bytes per element.`,
      follow:`How would you test for false sharing specifically?`,
      red:`Says threads always scale, or has no way to measure.` },
    { q:`You have two weeks to make a 10,000-agent simulation fit a mobile frame. How do you order the work?`,
      a:`Capture on the lowest device and set the budget. Remove algorithmic waste (pairs, recomputation). Slice what tolerates latency. Pack the hot data and cut pointer chasing. Then jobs and Burst for bulk loops. Keep benchmarks at 1x, 2x, 4x counts and guard percentiles. Say what you would not do without evidence: pooling cheap objects, hand SIMD, a full ECS migration.`,
      follow:`What is the first thing you cut if you must ship with less?`,
      red:`Starts with the most advanced technique, or rewrites with no baseline capture.` },
    { q:`Argue for and against adopting ECS (Unity Entities) for a game with 500 complex actors and 20,000 simple projectiles.`,
      a:`For the projectiles: same shape, bulk update, chunked arrays by archetype, jobs and Burst. Against the actors: few, heterogeneous, edited by designers, where the object workflow is clearer. A hybrid fits: ECS or plain packed arrays for projectiles, objects for actors. Costs: tooling, workflow, hiring, authoring conversion. Test with the projectile system only.`,
      follow:`Would plain packed arrays and a job do for the projectiles?`,
      red:`All or nothing.` }
  ] });
DIAGRAM('craft-cpu-cache-and-data-layout', { kind:'stack', title:'Memory hierarchy: each step down is slower and larger', arrow:'down',
  layers:[{t:'Registers', d:'Under 1 cycle, a few hundred bytes'},{t:'L1 cache', d:'About 4 cycles, 32 to 64 KB per core'},{t:'L2 cache', d:'About 12 to 15 cycles, hundreds of KB to a few MB'},{t:'L3 cache', d:'About 40 to 70 cycles, shared, megabytes'},{t:'Main memory', d:'Hundreds of cycles, about 70 to 100 ns idle'}],
  note:'Orders of magnitude for recent desktop cores; mobile differs. Memory moves in 64-byte lines. Sources: Drepper, Chips and Cheese, vendor manuals.' });
EXPLAINER('craft-cpu-cache-and-data-layout', { kind:'explainer', title:'One loop, two layouts: how many cache lines does it pull in?',
  frames:[
    { t:'The loop reads the position and velocity of 5,000 enemies', spec:{ kind:'matrix', rows:['Enemy struct','What the loop reads','Enemies'], cols:['Size','Note'], cells:[['128 bytes','position, velocity, name, loot, AI state'],['16 bytes of each','position 8 bytes and velocity 8 bytes'],['5,000','one pass every frame']] } },
    { t:'Array of structs: each read fetches a whole 64-byte line', d:'At most 16 bytes of each line are used; the rest is fetched and thrown away.', spec:{ kind:'matrix', rows:['Lines fetched','Bytes fetched','Bytes the loop uses'], cols:['Array of structs','Note'], cells:[['5,000 to 10,000','one or two lines per enemy'],['320 to 640 KB','the whole line, every time'],['80 KB','12 to 25% of what was fetched']] } },
    { t:'Split hot from cold', spec:{ kind:'stack', layers:[{ t:'Hot: positions[5000]', d:'8 bytes each, packed together' }, { t:'Hot: velocities[5000]', d:'8 bytes each, packed together' }, { t:'Cold: names, loot, AI state', d:'read only when one enemy is inspected' }] } },
    { t:'Structure of arrays: every fetched byte is used', d:'The loop walks two dense arrays, so the hardware prefetcher can run ahead of it.', spec:{ kind:'matrix', rows:['Lines fetched','Bytes fetched','Share of fetched bytes used','Access pattern'], cols:['Array of structs','Structure of arrays'], cells:[['5,000 to 10,000','1,250'],['320 to 640 KB','80 KB'],['12 to 25%','100%'],['a jump of 128 bytes per enemy','one dense stream']] } },
    { t:'Far fewer lines are fetched each frame. Measure to confirm', d:'A profiler\'s cache-miss counter, not this arithmetic, says whether it helped on your target.', spec:{ kind:'matrix', rows:['Working set per frame','L1 data cache, per core','L2 cache on a phone core'], cols:['Size','Typical'], cells:[['80 KB (was up to 640 KB)',''],['','32 to 64 KB'],['','256 KB to 2 MB']] } }
  ] });

T('craft-gpu-rendering-cost',{ d:'craft', t:'GPU cost: draw calls, overdraw, shaders and LOD', tag:'A frame is built in passes. The CPU pays per draw, the GPU pays per pixel and per vertex, and the fix for one does nothing for the other. Find which side is late, then cut the biggest line.',
  what:`Rendering a frame is a pipeline with two owners. The CPU decides what to draw: it culls objects, sorts them, and records commands (set this shader, bind this texture, draw this mesh). The GPU executes those commands: it runs the vertex shader per vertex, rasterises triangles into fragments, runs the fragment shader per covered pixel, tests depth, blends the result and writes it to a render target. The two run in parallel on different frames, so the slower one sets the frame time. Unreal’s stat unit lists the Game thread, the Draw (render) thread and the GPU separately and says a frame time close to one thread’s time points to that thread as the bottleneck. GPU time is synced to the frame, so it is often close to Frame time even when the GPU is not the limit; trust it only when it is clearly the longest figure. That gives two families of cost. CPU-side cost scales with the number of draws, state changes, objects culled and lights and shadow passes submitted. GPU-side cost scales with vertices shaded, pixels shaded times the work per pixel (overdraw times shader length), texture and render-target bandwidth, and the number of full-screen passes. The tricks of the trade follow from that split. Batching, instancing and indirect draws cut CPU cost. Culling, LOD and impostors cut both vertices and draws. Fewer transparent layers, cheaper shaders, lower resolution and fewer or smaller shadow passes cut GPU cost. Texture compression and baked lighting cut memory bandwidth, which on a phone also means heat and battery. Frame order matters too: a frame is made of passes (shadow maps, an optional depth pre-pass, opaque, alpha-tested, transparent, post-processing and upscale, then UI), and a cost always belongs to one pass, which is how a capture tool shows it. Everything here is a lens to test on the target device. A scene with 200 draws and one full-screen shader is not a batching problem, and a scene with 5,000 draws of a cheap shader is not a fill-rate problem.`,
  why:[`The frame budget is shared by CPU and GPU in parallel, so a fix aimed at the wrong side changes nothing. Cutting draw calls on a frame limited by fill rate is the commonest wasted week in rendering work.`,`Fill cost multiplies. A 1,920 by 1,080 target is 2,073,600 pixels, or about 124 million pixels a second at 60 fps for a single layer. Three overlapping layers of a full-screen effect make it about 373 million, before the shader length is counted.`,`Resolution is the cheapest large lever. A render scale of 0.67 on each axis shades about 45 percent of the pixels (by the same arithmetic, 50 percent on each axis shades a quarter of the pixels).`,`On phones the GPU shares memory bandwidth and a thermal budget with the CPU. Bandwidth-heavy techniques (large uncompressed textures, many full-screen passes, heavy blending) cost battery and heat as well as milliseconds, and that is what throttles a game after ten minutes (see craft-performance for the thermal test).`,`Art decisions set the render cost long before a programmer sees it: triangle counts, how many materials a character has, whether grass is transparent cards, how many dynamic lights a room has. A budget written for artists is worth more than any optimisation pass after the content exists.`],
  think:{ q:[`Which side is late: the CPU main thread, the render thread, or the GPU? What did you measure to know?`,`If you halve the render resolution, does frame time move? If it does, you are fill or bandwidth bound; if not, look at draws, vertices or the CPU.`,`How many draw calls and state changes are in the worst scene, and how many are the same mesh and material repeated?`,`What is the overdraw in the worst scene: how many times is an average pixel shaded, and where are the hot spots (particles, foliage, UI, glass)?`,`Which pass is the largest on the GPU: shadows, opaque, transparent, post-processing?`,`How long is the fragment shader for the most common material, and does it read many textures?`,`How many shader variants does the build contain, and how many does a scene use?`,`Which of this scene’s objects could not be seen from the camera positions the design allows?`,`What does each texture cost in memory, in the format it ships in on each platform?`],
    trade:[`Static batching merges meshes and cuts draws, at the price of extra CPU memory for the combined meshes. Unity’s manual says each static batch buffer holds up to 64,000 vertices, and that static batching is the method to use when you want Unity to cull static meshes individually; it is meshes you combine by hand that can no longer be culled one by one.`,`Dynamic batching trades CPU time to merge small meshes every frame. Unity’s manual says it is no longer recommended, because the CPU overhead can exceed the cost of a draw call, and limits it to meshes under 300 vertices and 900 vertex attributes.`,`GPU instancing and Godot’s MultiMesh draw thousands of copies in one call, at the cost of one shared mesh and material, and in Godot’s case no per-instance culling: the whole set is culled or drawn as one.`,`Alpha blending gives soft edges and costs overdraw and sorting. Alpha test (Godot’s alpha scissor) is faster than blending in Godot’s docs and gives hard edges that need MSAA with alpha-to-coverage or dithering to look right. It is not always cheaper: measure on the device, because on tile-based mobile GPUs discard can defeat hidden-surface removal (judgement, not sourced to a vendor page here).`,`Real-time lights and shadows give moving light and cost shadow passes; baked light costs memory and iteration time and no frame time. Godot’s docs advise baked lighting for mobile.`,`Dynamic resolution and upscalers trade sharpness for GPU time and add a post-process pass of their own. Temporal ones add history buffers and ghosting risk.`,`Lower LODs save vertices and cost memory, authoring time and visible pops. Impostors (a flat picture of a distant object) cut cost most and look worst at close range.`,`Compressed textures save memory and bandwidth and cost quality, and each platform wants a different format.`],
    traps:[`Reducing draw calls on a frame that is fill-rate bound.`,`Reducing triangle count on a frame whose cost is a full-screen post-process or three layers of transparent particles.`,`Checking performance in the editor on a desktop GPU and shipping to a phone, where the same scene is bandwidth bound.`,`Making everything transparent “to look soft”: grass cards, hair, fog sheets and UI glow layers stack overdraw that no draw-call count shows.`,`Turning on dynamic batching for a project that already uses the SRP Batcher or instancing, and paying CPU for nothing.`,`Using a MaterialPropertyBlock per object on materials that would otherwise batch: any MaterialPropertyBlock makes a GameObject incompatible with the SRP Batcher (Unity’s manual). Check in the Frame Debugger which batches broke; its Batch cause field explains SRP Batcher breaks only.`,`Shipping uncompressed textures because they look right in the editor. A 2,048 by 2,048 RGBA8 texture is 16 MiB, about 21.3 MiB with mips.`,`Counting light count without counting shadow-casting lights: each shadow-casting light, and each directional-light cascade, is another pass over the casters.`,`Letting shader variants multiply (keywords times passes times platforms) until builds are long, memory is high and the first use of each variant hitches while it compiles.`,`Reading one frame in a GPU capture and fixing a cost that only exists in that frame.`,`Assuming a 3 ms GPU pass is cheap: on a 16.7 ms frame it is 18 percent.`],
    good:[`The team has a written render budget per platform: draws, triangles on screen, overdraw, shadow-casting lights, texture memory, and the GPU milliseconds each pass may take.`,`Every material has a known shader cost class and artists can see it before they commit.`,`Transparent effects are budgeted by screen area and layer count, not by particle count.`,`A GPU capture of the worst scene is taken on the target phone for every milestone, and the top three passes have owners.`],
    bad:[`“The GPU is slow” with no pass-level capture.`,`Optimisation by turning settings down until the editor graph looks green.`,`Nobody can say how many shadow-casting lights or cascades the build uses.`] },
  how:[`Decide whether you are CPU or GPU bound before touching anything. In Unreal run stat unit and compare Game, Draw and GPU against Frame. In Unity compare the Profiler’s CPU timeline with GPU time on a device that reports it. In Godot read the Debugger’s Visual Profiler, which graphs rendering CPU time on the left and GPU time on the right. Then run the resolution test: drop the render scale to 0.5 and see whether frame time falls. A large fall means fill or bandwidth bound.`,`Set render budgets on paper first. For a 60 fps phone game, write numbers for draws, on-screen triangles, overdraw factor, shadow-casting lights, texture memory and milliseconds per pass. Start from what the lowest supported phone can hold after ten minutes of play, not from the flagship.`,`Capture one frame with a graphics debugger and read it pass by pass. Use RenderDoc (Vulkan, D3D11, D3D12, OpenGL and OpenGL ES, on Windows, Linux, Android and Nintendo Switch), PIX for D3D12 on Windows (Xbox PIX ships with the GDK; not confirmed on a public page), Xcode’s Metal debugger and GPU frame capture on Apple devices (named from practice; Apple’s page was not readable here), and the Unity Frame Debugger to step through draws. In Unreal use stat gpu for a live view and profilegpu for one frame’s breakdown. Write down, for each pass, its draws and its milliseconds.`,`If the CPU side is late, count draws and state changes first. In Unity, use the Frame Debugger; with the SRP Batcher in use its Batch cause field says why an event could not be batched (for example a MaterialPropertyBlock or an incompatible shader). Keep materials few, share them across objects, and let the engine batch: the SRP Batcher in URP and HDRP, GPU instancing for many copies of one mesh and material, static batching for scenery that never moves. In Godot use MultiMeshInstance3D for thousands of copies of one mesh, and note its all-or-nothing culling: split a field into several MultiMeshes by area so whole tiles can be culled.`,`For huge counts of one mesh (foliage, debris, crowds), consider indirect drawing: a compute shader culls instances on the GPU and writes the draw arguments to a buffer, and one call draws the survivors (Unity: Graphics.RenderMeshIndirect, which needs compute shader support). It removes CPU culling cost and adds a compute pass and a harder debugging story. Do it only when a capture shows the CPU culling or draw submission as the cost.`,`Cull aggressively before the GPU sees anything. Frustum culling is automatic. Add distance culling (a maximum draw distance per object class), and occlusion culling for dense indoor or city scenes, where Godot’s documentation notes a naive renderer in a town can try to render 10 or 100 times more than is visible. The gain is mostly in draws, vertices and shadow work for hidden objects, because a depth pre-pass already keeps hidden pixels from being fully shaded. Bake or place occluders from the level’s big solid shapes (walls, floors, buildings), never from small props. Check the culling result with the engine’s debug view before trusting it.`,`For vertex cost, add LODs. Author three or four levels per hero asset and swap by screen size, not distance, so a zoom or a field-of-view change does not break it. Use HLOD (merge a distant cluster into one low-detail mesh) for buildings and set dressing, and an impostor or flat card for distant vegetation. Put a hard cull on the last level. Check the seam between levels by walking the camera slowly in the worst case.`,`For overdraw, view it. RenderDoc and most engine debug views can show how many times each pixel was shaded. Then reduce it: draw opaque objects front to back so early depth rejection works (engines sort for you, but check that your custom passes do); replace blended foliage and hair cards with alpha test or alpha-to-coverage (measure it: alpha test and discard can defeat early depth rejection, as NVIDIA’s GPU Gems chapter on pipeline performance warns, and on tile-based mobile GPUs discard can cost more than blending); trim transparent sprite and particle quads to the visible shape (a quad with a large transparent border still shades every pixel); shrink big particles; and keep UI glow layers to a few. A common practice for translucent meshes is to split them so the opaque core draws front to back and only the small translucent edge draws back to front.`,`For fragment shader cost, count what each pixel does: texture fetches, maths and branches. Arithmetic on a GPU is cheap relative to fetches and bandwidth, and a long chain of texture reads (base, normal, mask, detail, lightmap) is often the real cost. Pack masks into the channels of one texture, drop detail layers on distant surfaces, use fewer or lower-precision operations on mobile (half precision where banding is not visible), and use a lighter shader for objects that are small on screen. AMD’s occupancy article explains that fewer vector registers per shader let more waves run at once, which hides memory latency: a shader that is simpler for the compiler often runs faster than one with more outputs than it needs.`,`Control variants. Strip keywords the game never uses, use shader features chosen at build time for rarely changing options, and keep runtime keywords to a handful. Prewarm the variants the game will need during a loading screen so the first explosion does not stall on a compile.`,`Handle resolution on purpose. Pick the render scale per device tier. Use dynamic resolution where the platform supports it: Unreal’s heuristic scales the primary screen percentage from the previous frames’ GPU load and defaults to a minimum of 50 and a maximum of 100. In Godot set Viewport scaling_3d_mode and scaling_3d_scale; below 1.0 renders at lower resolution, and AMD’s FSR presets are 0.77, 0.67, 0.59 and 0.5. Keep UI and text at native resolution so they stay sharp.`,`Budget lights and shadows. Prefer baked lighting for static scenery. Limit shadow-casting lights to what the camera can see at once. For the sun, use cascaded shadow maps with as few cascades as the view needs: each cascade is another shadow map drawn from the scene’s casters. Microsoft’s write-up notes that geometry with a high dynamic range in depth (such as a camera low to the ground with far views) needs more cascades, and an overhead view needs few. Fix the cascade split distances per scene (shifting them per frame makes shadow edges shimmer, per the same article), and cull shadow casters that are small or far.`,`Compress textures and set mip maps. Use ASTC on current iOS and Android devices (a block is always 128 bits, so 4 by 4 is 8 bits per pixel, 6 by 6 is about 3.6, 8 by 8 is 2, and 12 by 12 is about 0.89), BC7 or DXT on desktop, ETC2 as the older Android fallback. Pick the block size per texture: normal maps and faces keep 4 by 4, distant terrain and masks can take 8 by 8. Keep power-of-two or block-aligned sizes. Turn mip maps on for 3D textures (they cost a third more memory and save bandwidth and shimmer) and off for UI.`,`Re-capture. Compare the same scene at the same camera, on the same device, with the same settings, and keep both captures with the change. Add the worst scene to a benchmark that records GPU time per pass, so a new shader or a new effect that costs 2 ms shows up in the build that introduced it.`],
  ai:{ yes:[`Read a RenderDoc, PIX or engine-profiler export and list the top passes, with the likely cause of each (draws, fill, bandwidth, shader).`,`Count draws, batches and state changes from a frame-debugger listing and say why batches broke.`,`Estimate texture memory for a list of textures at a given format and mip setting.`,`Write a shader variant strip script or a keyword audit for a project.`,`Draft a render budget table for a platform from the numbers you give it.`],
       no:[`Tell you where the frame goes without a capture from your device. GPU cost depends on the exact chip, driver and resolution.`,`Know a phone’s real fill rate, bandwidth or thermal limit. Take those from the vendor or measure.`,`Judge whether lowering a LOD distance or a render scale looks acceptable. That is an art and design review on a device.`,`Decide the budget split between passes. That is a negotiation between art, design and engineering.`] },
  prompts:[{l:'Read this GPU capture',p:`Target: [DEVICE], [FPS] fps, so [MS] ms per frame. Here is the pass-by-pass listing from [TOOL]: [PASTE]. For each pass give the milliseconds and share of the frame, whether it looks bound by draws, fill, bandwidth or shader length, and the one measurement that would confirm it. Rank the top five. Do not suggest changes to anything under 5 percent of the frame.`},
    {l:'Texture memory budget',p:`Here is a list of textures with width, height, channels, current format and whether mips are on: [PASTE]. Target platforms: [PLATFORMS]. Compute memory per texture and in total as shipped, and again with ASTC at the block size you recommend for each kind (colour, normal, mask, UI). Show the arithmetic for three of them and say which textures I should look at by eye before accepting the lower quality.`},
    {l:'Why did the batch break?',p:`This is a frame debugger listing from [ENGINE]: [PASTE]. Group the draws by mesh and material. For each run of repeated draws that did not batch, say the most likely reason from the listing and what I should change (shared material, instancing, MaterialPropertyBlock, shader compatibility). Do not guess about anything the listing does not show; list what extra information you need.`}],
  verify:[`Is there a capture from the target device, at the target resolution, before and after, in the same scene and camera position?`,`Did you check which side was late (CPU or GPU) before choosing the fix?`,`Does the change reduce the number you aimed at (draws, overdraw, pass milliseconds), and not only the total frame time?`,`Did culling or LOD changes remove anything visible? Walk the camera on the design’s worst routes with the debug view on.`,`Do batched or instanced objects still look right: lighting, per-object colour, shadows, lightmaps?`,`Do compressed textures look acceptable on the phone, in motion, at the distance the player sees them? Check normal maps and gradients for banding.`,`Is shader variant count and build time unchanged or smaller, and is first use of each effect free of a hitch?`,`Did the GPU time stay flat after ten minutes on a phone, or does it climb as the device heats?`],
  test:[`Capture the worst scene at 1.0, 0.75 and 0.5 render scale on the target phone. GPU time that falls with the pixel count is fill bound; one that barely moves is not.`,`Run the same camera path with transparency, then with transparent materials swapped for opaque ones, and compare GPU time. The gap is your overdraw bill.`,`Place the camera at the densest point of each level and record draws, triangles and GPU milliseconds per pass. Compare each with the budget.`,`Toggle shadows, post-processing and the biggest particle system one at a time and record the change in GPU time on the device.`,`Fly slowly through each LOD transition in a normal build, with the camera and field of view the game uses, and look for pops or missing objects.`,`Play thirty minutes on the lowest supported phone and plot GPU frame time, battery and temperature. A rising line is thermal throttling, not a code regression.`,`Repeat the capture after every large content drop, with the same scene, so new art is measured against the budget when it lands.`],
  facts:[{claim:`Unreal’s stat unit shows Frame, Game, Draw, GPU, RHIT and DynRes times; the documentation says frame time is likely bottlenecked by the game thread or by the rendering thread when it is about equal to that thread’s time.`,asOf:'2026-10-05',src:'https://dev.epicgames.com/documentation/en-us/unreal-engine/stat-commands-in-unreal-engine'},
    {claim:`Unreal’s Dynamic Resolution adjusts the primary screen percentage from the previous frames’ GPU workload; r.DynamicRes.MinScreenPercentage defaults to 50, MaxScreenPercentage to 100 and FrameTimeBudget to 33.3 ms; it is supported on current consoles and on PC with DX12 or Vulkan.`,asOf:'2026-10-05',src:'https://dev.epicgames.com/documentation/en-us/unreal-engine/dynamic-resolution-in-unreal-engine'},
    {claim:`Unity’s manual says the SRP Batcher reduces render-state changes between draw calls rather than the number of draw calls; it works in URP and HDRP and not in the Built-in Render Pipeline.`,asOf:'2026-10-05',src:'https://docs.unity3d.com/6000.0/Documentation/Manual/SRPBatcher.html'},
    {claim:`Unity’s dynamic batching (not supported in HDRP) limits meshes to 300 vertices and 900 vertex attributes and the manual says it is no longer recommended because the CPU overhead can exceed the cost of a draw call; a static batch buffer holds up to 64,000 vertices; the manual says to use static batching when you want static meshes culled individually.`,asOf:'2026-10-05',src:'https://docs.unity3d.com/6000.0/Documentation/Manual/DrawCallBatching.html'},
    {claim:`Unity’s GPU instancing draws many GameObjects that share a mesh and material in one draw call; Skinned Mesh Renderers are not supported, and with URP or HDRP custom shaders need the SRP Batcher disabled or the shader made incompatible with it.`,asOf:'2026-10-05',src:'https://docs.unity3d.com/6000.0/Documentation/Manual/GPUInstancing.html'},
    {claim:`Graphics.RenderMeshIndirect renders instances using draw arguments read from a graphics buffer and only works on platforms that support compute shaders.`,asOf:'2026-10-05',src:'https://docs.unity3d.com/6000.0/Documentation/ScriptReference/Graphics.RenderMeshIndirect.html'},
    {claim:`Godot’s MultiMesh draws thousands of instances in one draw call, and the manual states no screen or frustum culling is possible for individual instances: the whole MultiMesh is culled with one bounding box.`,asOf:'2026-10-05',src:'https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/tutorials/performance/using_multimesh.html'},
    {claim:`Godot’s Visual Profiler graphs rendering CPU time and GPU time by category, and shows rendering CPU time on the left and GPU time on the right.`,asOf:'2026-10-05',src:'https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/tutorials/scripting/debug/debugger_panel.html'},
    {claim:`Godot’s Viewport scaling_3d_scale below 1.0 renders 3D at lower resolution; modes include bilinear, FSR 1.0, FSR 2.2 and MetalFX; AMD’s recommended FSR presets are 0.77, 0.67, 0.59 and 0.5.`,asOf:'2026-10-05',src:'https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class_viewport.html'},
    {claim:`Unity recommends ASTC on iOS and on Android devices with OpenGL ES 3.1 or Vulkan, ranging from 8 bits per pixel (4 by 4) to 0.89 bits per pixel (12 by 12); on desktop DXT1 at 4 bits per pixel and BC7 or DXT5 at 8.`,asOf:'2026-10-05',src:'https://docs.unity3d.com/6000.0/Documentation/Manual/texture-choose-format-by-platform.html'},
    {claim:`RenderDoc is a graphics debugger for Vulkan, D3D11, D3D12, OpenGL and OpenGL ES on Windows, Linux, Android and Nintendo Switch.`,asOf:'2026-10-05',src:'https://renderdoc.org/docs/index.html'}],
  rel:[['craft-performance','The frame budget and the CPU or GPU bound check live there; this topic is the rendering half of what it tells you to fix.'],['craft-cpu-cache-and-data-layout','Draw submission, culling and instance arrays are CPU work; the layout and slicing tricks there apply to the culling loop.'],['visual-language','Style sets the overdraw, the transparency and the shader count; clarity rules decide what can be cut without losing readability.'],['animation-and-vfx','Particles and screen effects are the usual overdraw and fill-rate cost.'],['level-blockout-and-metrics','Sight lines, room sizes and occluder shapes in the blockout decide what culling can remove.'],['camera-design','Field of view, distance and angle decide LOD ranges, shadow cascades and what is visible at once.']] });
TECH('craft-gpu-rendering-cost',[
  {n:'SRP Batcher (Unity URP and HDRP)', how:'Keep shaders compatible, use few shader variants, and give each object its own material properties through per-material constant buffers that stay on the GPU. The SRP Batcher then cuts render-state changes between draws.', fit:'Scenes with many different materials on one or a few shaders, which is most URP and HDRP games.', cost:'Shaders must be compatible, and features that bypass it (any MaterialPropertyBlock) break the batch. It does not reduce the draw count, only the state changes between draws.', alt:'GPU instancing for many identical objects; static batching for scenery.'},
  {n:'GPU instancing or MultiMesh', how:'Draw many copies of one mesh and material in a single call, with per-instance transform and optional colour. Unity: an instancing-enabled material and Graphics.RenderMeshInstanced. Godot: MultiMeshInstance3D.', fit:'Foliage, rocks, debris, crowds of identical units, particles built from meshes.', cost:'One mesh and material per call. In Godot the whole MultiMesh is culled as one box, so split big fields into tiles. Unity instancing does not support skinned meshes.', alt:'Static batching if the objects never move; indirect draws when the count is very large and a GPU cull pays.'},
  {n:'Indirect draws with GPU culling', how:'A compute shader tests each instance against the frustum (and optionally a depth pyramid), appends survivors to a buffer, and writes the draw arguments. One indirect draw renders them. Unity: Graphics.RenderMeshIndirect.', fit:'Tens of thousands of instances where CPU culling or submission shows in the capture: vegetation, large simulations.', cost:'Needs compute support, a compute pass of its own, and is harder to debug. Little gain below a few thousand instances.', alt:'CPU culling by tile with instancing; fewer, larger objects.'},
  {n:'Static and dynamic batching', how:'Static: mark non-moving meshes so the engine merges them into shared buffers at build or load. Dynamic: the engine merges small meshes each frame on the CPU.', fit:'Static batching for scenery on pipelines without a better path or on low-end devices. Dynamic only on the lowest-end devices after a capture, and not in HDRP.', cost:'Static costs extra CPU memory for the merged meshes; Unity says it is the method to pick when you want static meshes culled individually (combining by hand is what loses per-object culling). Dynamic batching is not supported in HDRP. Dynamic costs CPU every frame, and Unity says it is no longer recommended.', alt:'SRP Batcher, instancing, or fewer materials through texture atlases.'},
  {n:'Texture atlases and material sharing', how:'Pack many small textures (UI icons, props, sprites) into one atlas and share one material across them, so draws are not split by texture.', fit:'Sprites, UI, modular props and 2D games, where the draw count is set by how many textures are bound.', cost:'Atlas bleeding at edges, mip problems, packing effort, and a larger single texture in memory even when one icon is used.', alt:'Texture arrays or bindless access where the platform supports them.'},
  {n:'Alpha test, alpha-to-coverage and trimmed quads instead of blending', how:'Use a cut-out for foliage and hair with MSAA alpha-to-coverage or dithering, and mesh-fit transparent sprites so the quad has little empty area.', fit:'Foliage, fences, hair, particle and sprite-heavy scenes with high overdraw.', cost:'Harder edges, noise or shimmer, and alpha test and discard can disable early depth rejection (NVIDIA’s GPU Gems chapter; on tile-based mobile GPUs it can defeat hidden-surface removal, so measure on the device). Needs MSAA or temporal smoothing to look right. Godot’s docs say alpha scissor is faster than blending.', alt:'Order-independent transparency, which Godot’s docs call costly and does not provide.'},
  {n:'Dynamic resolution and spatial or temporal upscalers', how:'Render the 3D scene at a scale below 1.0, chosen per frame from GPU load or per device tier, then upscale (bilinear, FSR 1.0 spatial, FSR 2.2 or MetalFX temporal in Godot). Keep UI at native resolution.', fit:'GPU-bound games with variable scene cost, and consoles and phones where fill rate is the limit.', cost:'Softer image, scale changes can be visible, temporal methods need motion vectors and history and can ghost, and the upscale pass has its own cost. On a CPU-bound frame it saves nothing.', alt:'A fixed per-tier render scale; cut the heaviest effect.'},
  {n:'LOD, HLOD and impostors', how:'Swap a mesh for a cheaper one at set screen sizes, merge distant clusters into one proxy, and draw far objects as flat cards.', fit:'Open worlds and any scene whose vertex cost or draw count comes from distant objects.', cost:'Authoring time, memory for each level, visible pops, and bad shadow and silhouette behaviour if shadow casters use different levels.', alt:'Culling by distance; fewer, simpler assets.'},
  {n:'Occlusion culling', how:'Mark big static solids as occluders and the objects behind them as occludees; the engine skips what is hidden. Godot supports occluder nodes; Unity bakes occlusion data.', fit:'Interiors, cities, and any scene with many walls hiding much of the world.', cost:'Bake time, memory for the data, CPU time for the visibility test, and no gain in open scenes. A bad occluder hides things that should be visible.', alt:'Portals or manual room visibility; frustum and distance culling only.'},
  {n:'Baked lighting with a few real-time lights', how:'Bake static lights and indirect light into lightmaps and probes, and keep real-time lights for moving objects and a single shadow-casting sun.', fit:'Mobile and any game with a static world.', cost:'Memory for lightmaps, iteration time, and no response to moving geometry. Godot advises baked lighting for mobile.', alt:'Fully dynamic light on high-end targets; fake blob shadows under characters.'},
  {n:'Tuned shadow cascades and shadow distance', how:'Choose the cascade count and split distances for the camera in this game, fix them per scene, and cap the shadow distance. Cull shadow casters that are small or far.', fit:'Every game with a sun and a perspective camera.', cost:'Each cascade is another shadow map drawn from the casters; fewer cascades or a short distance give blocky or missing shadows far out.', alt:'Baked shadows for distance, with cascades only near the camera.'},
  {n:'Compressed textures with per-texture block size', how:'Ship ASTC on current mobile (block sizes 4 by 4 to 12 by 12, always 128 bits), BC7 or DXT on desktop, ETC2 as an older Android fallback, and choose block size per texture type.', fit:'Every project. Texture memory and bandwidth are among the first things to cut on phones.', cost:'Compression artefacts, longer import, and a different format per platform. Normal maps and gradients show banding sooner than colour.', alt:'Smaller uncompressed textures for tiny UI or pixel art that must stay exact.'},
  {n:'Shader variant stripping and prewarming', how:'Remove keywords and passes the game never uses at build time, then compile the needed variants in a loading screen so no variant compiles on first use in play.', fit:'Any project with many materials, platforms or render features.', cost:'A stripped variant that is needed at runtime renders wrong or pink. Prewarming costs load time.', alt:'Fewer shader features; shader graphs with one fixed path.'}]);
ENGINE('craft-gpu-rendering-cost',{
  unity:{ term:`Unity gives you the Frame Debugger to step through draws and see why batches broke, the Profiler for CPU and GPU time, and in URP and HDRP the SRP Batcher. For many copies of one mesh, Graphics.RenderMeshInstanced draws up to an array of matrices in few calls using GPU instancing; Graphics.RenderMeshIndirect takes its arguments from a GPU buffer.`,
    api:['Graphics.RenderMeshInstanced','RenderParams','Matrix4x4.TRS','Frame Debugger','SRP Batcher','Graphics.RenderMeshIndirect'],
    snippet:`using UnityEngine;

public class Grass : MonoBehaviour {
    [SerializeField] Mesh mesh;
    [SerializeField] Material material;   // Enable GPU Instancing
    Matrix4x4[] m = new Matrix4x4[500];
    void Start() {
        for (int i = 0; i < m.Length; i++)
            m[i] = Matrix4x4.TRS(new Vector3(i % 40, 0, i / 40),
                                 Quaternion.identity, Vector3.one);
    }
    void Update() {
        Graphics.RenderMeshInstanced(new RenderParams(material), mesh, 0, m);
    }
}`,
    pitfall:`Assuming instancing is on because the code ran. The material and shader must support it (the manual lists the requirements), and the way to know is to look. Open the Frame Debugger, step to the grass, and check the instanced draws and their instance counts. Unity caps one instanced draw at 1,023 instances, and 511 by default because each instance carries two matrices (objectToWorld and worldToObject), so 500 here fits one draw and 1,000 would need at least two unless the shader uses assumeuniformscaling. Also keep in mind that GPU instancing is not for skinned meshes, and that for GameObjects with Mesh Renderers in URP the SRP Batcher takes precedence unless the shader is incompatible with it; a direct RenderMeshInstanced call uses instancing explicitly.`,
    map:`Graphics.RenderMeshInstanced is the Unity form of Godot’s MultiMesh; the Frame Debugger has no direct Godot editor equivalent, so use RenderDoc to see the draw list in Godot.` },
  godot:{ term:`Godot draws many identical meshes in one call with MultiMesh. The Debugger’s Visual Profiler graphs rendering CPU and GPU time by category, and the Monitors tab shows draw calls. For a graphics debugger, use RenderDoc or a platform tool. The renderer batches some identical objects automatically in Forward+, but MultiMesh is the explicit tool for thousands.`,
    api:['MultiMeshInstance3D','MultiMesh.instance_count','MultiMesh.set_instance_transform()','MultiMesh.visible_instance_count','MultiMesh.transform_format','BoxMesh'],
    snippet:`extends MultiMeshInstance3D

func _ready() -> void:
	var mm := MultiMesh.new()
	mm.transform_format = MultiMesh.TRANSFORM_3D
	mm.mesh = BoxMesh.new()
	mm.instance_count = 10000
	for i in mm.instance_count:
		var p := Vector3(i % 100, 0.0, floorf(i / 100.0))
		mm.set_instance_transform(i, Transform3D(Basis(), p))
	multimesh = mm
	# mm.visible_instance_count = 5000   # draw only the first 5,000`,
    pitfall:`The whole MultiMesh is culled with one bounding box: if any part is on screen, all 10,000 instances are submitted. For a big field, make one MultiMeshInstance3D per tile so tiles outside the view are skipped. Set transform_format and instance_count before writing transforms, because setting the count resizes the buffers.`,
    map:`A MultiMesh is Unity’s GPU instancing through Graphics.RenderMeshInstanced; Godot’s visible_instance_count plays the role of passing a smaller count to the Unity call.` },
  note:`Unreal follows the same logic with its own tools: stat unit to find the late side, stat gpu and profilegpu for pass costs, instanced static meshes and hierarchical LODs for draws, and dynamic resolution for fill. In every engine the method is the same: find the late side, capture a frame, read it pass by pass, cut the biggest cost and capture again.` });
INTERVIEW('craft-gpu-rendering-cost',{
  junior:[
    { q:`A game runs at 40 fps and you need 60. How do you tell whether the CPU or the GPU is the problem?`,
      a:`Compare CPU thread times with GPU time in the profiler (stat unit in Unreal; the Profiler in Unity; the Visual Profiler in Godot). The longest sets the frame, and GPU time is synced to the frame, so it only means the GPU is the limit when it is clearly the longest. Then lower the render scale: a big drop in frame time means fill or bandwidth bound.`,
      follow:`Which fix would you try for each?`,
      red:`Starts removing polygons or draw calls without measuring.` },
    { q:`What is a draw call, and why can many of them slow a game on a phone?`,
      a:`A command from the CPU telling the GPU to draw a mesh with the current state. Each carries driver and state-change work on the CPU, so thousands can use up the render thread even when the GPU is idle. Fix by batching, instancing and sharing materials. Say that the cost is per call, not per triangle.`,
      follow:`When does reducing them not help?`,
      red:`Thinks draw calls and triangle count are the same thing.` },
    { q:`What is overdraw?`,
      a:`The number of times an average pixel is shaded in a frame. Transparent layers, particles and foliage cards stack it. Cost is pixels times overdraw times shader length, so a full-screen layer on a high-resolution phone is expensive.`,
      follow:`Name two ways to reduce it.`,
      red:`Describes it as drawing objects off screen.` }
  ],
  mid:[
    { q:`Explain static batching, dynamic batching, the SRP Batcher and GPU instancing. When would you use each?`,
      a:`Static merges non-moving meshes in advance (memory cost). Dynamic merges small meshes on the CPU each frame; Unity says no longer recommended, only for low-end, and HDRP does not support it. SRP Batcher cuts state changes between draws with persistent material buffers. Instancing draws many copies of one mesh and material in one call. Scenery gets static or the SRP Batcher; copies get instancing; check the Frame Debugger for what actually happened.`,
      follow:`A MaterialPropertyBlock on each object: what does it do to those?`,
      red:`Says all four reduce draw calls in the same way.` },
    { q:`How would you cut GPU cost in a scene full of transparent foliage?`,
      a:`Find the overdraw with a capture view. Convert blended cards to alpha test or alpha-to-coverage (measure on the phone, since discard can cost more than blending on tile-based GPUs), trim the quads to the leaf shape, add LODs that replace cards with solid meshes or impostors at distance, reduce layers at the camera, and check the shader length. Re-capture on the phone.`,
      follow:`What artefacts does alpha test bring?`,
      red:`Reduces the resolution of the whole game first.` },
    { q:`Why does a shadow-casting light cost more than a lit one?`,
      a:`It needs a shadow map drawn from the casters, so the scene is rendered again from the light, and the receiving shader samples that map. A directional light with cascades does this once per cascade. Cost scales with casters in the light’s view, not only with screen pixels.`,
      follow:`How would you cap it on a phone?`,
      red:`Thinks shadows only cost shader time.` },
    { q:`How do you choose a texture format for iOS, Android and desktop, and what does it save?`,
      a:`ASTC on current iOS and Android, with block size by use (4 by 4 for normals and faces, larger for distant surfaces), BC7 or DXT on desktop, ETC2 as an old Android fallback. A 2,048 by 2,048 RGBA8 texture is 16 MiB, and ASTC 4 by 4 is 4 MiB, so memory and bandwidth drop by a factor of four before mips.`,
      follow:`What goes wrong with compressed normal maps?`,
      red:`Does not know compression is decoded in hardware and reduces bandwidth.` }
  ],
  senior:[
    { q:`Design a render budget for a 60 fps phone game, and say how you would enforce it.`,
      a:`Start from the lowest supported phone after ten minutes of heat. Split 16.7 ms across CPU and GPU with headroom; set draws, on-screen triangles, overdraw, shadow-casting lights, texture memory and per-pass GPU time. Give each item an owner. A benchmark scene records GPU time per pass in CI, thresholds fail the build, and art gets the budget as a checklist (materials per character, particle screen area, texture sizes).`,
      follow:`Art goes over budget by 20 percent the week before launch. What do you cut first?`,
      red:`Budgets only for triangles, nothing for fill, bandwidth, or heat.` },
    { q:`Compare dynamic resolution with a fixed render scale per device tier. When does each one fail?`,
      a:`Dynamic adjusts to load and holds frame rate through heavy scenes, but scale changes can be visible and it needs a GPU-bound frame to help (it saves nothing on a CPU-bound one). Fixed scale is predictable and QA-friendly, and wastes headroom or misses spikes. Mix: fixed per tier, dynamic as a safety net with limits (Unreal defaults to 50 to 100 percent). Keep UI native. Temporal upscalers add motion-vector requirements and ghosting.`,
      follow:`How would you test that the heuristic does not oscillate?`,
      red:`Treats an upscaler as free.` },
    { q:`A scene has 20,000 grass clumps and the CPU render thread is the bottleneck. Walk through the options.`,
      a:`Confirm it is CPU-side (stat unit or Profiler). Instancing or MultiMesh to cut draws; tile the field so culling works per tile; LOD or density falloff with distance. If CPU culling and submission still show, move culling to a compute shader with indirect draws, accepting compute support, debugging cost and a less portable path. Keep overdraw in mind: grass cards stack alpha.`,
      follow:`What does tiling cost, and how do you pick the tile size?`,
      red:`Jumps to indirect draws before checking whether plain instancing is enough.` }
  ] });
